         </block>
      </static-content>
      <static-content flow-name="xsl-region-after-left">
         <block text-align="start" font-size="10pt">
            <page-number/>
         </block>
      </static-content>
      <static-content flow-name="xsl-region-after-first">
         <block font-size="10pt" text-align="end">
            <page-number/>
         </block>
      </static-content>
      <static-content flow-name="xsl-footnote-separator">
         <block>
            <leader leader-pattern="rule"
                    leader-length="100%"
                    rule-style="solid"
                    rule-thickness="0.5pt"/>
         </block>
      </static-content>
      <flow flow-name="xsl-region-body" font-family="Times" font-size="10pt">
         <block keep-with-next.within-page="always"
                id="Simple-eg"
                text-align="left"
                font-family="Times"
                font-size="18pt"
                font-weight="bold"
                space-after="6pt"
                space-before.optimum="12pt"
                text-indent="0em">1. A Short Example</block>
         <block line-height="1">We begin with a short example. How should we go about transferring into a computer a passage of prose, such as the start of the last chapter of Charlotte Brontë's novel <inline font-style="italic">Jane Eyre</inline>? We might start by simply copying what we see on the printed page, typing it in such a way that what appears on the screen looks as similar as possible, for example, by retaining the original line breaks, by introducing blanks to represent the layout of the original headings, page breaks, and paragraphs, and so forth. Of course, the possibilities are limited by the nature of the computer program we use to capture the text: it may not be possible for example to reflect accurately the typographic characteristics of our source with all such software. Some characters in the printed text (such as the accented letter <inline font-style="italic" font-weight="normal">a</inline> in <inline font-style="italic" font-weight="normal">faàl</inline> or the long dash) may not be available on the keyboard; some typographic distinctions (such as that between small capitals and full capitals) may not be readily accessible. Our first attempt tries to mimic the appearance of the former, and simply ignores the latter.</block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">
            <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">
                                CHAPTER 38

READER, I married him. A quiet wedding we had: he and I, the par-
son and clerk, were alone present. When we got back from church, I
went into the kitchen of the manor-house, where Mary was cooking
the dinner, and John cleaning the knives, and I said --
  'Mary, I have been married to Mr Rochester this morning.' The
housekeeper and her husband were of that decent, phlegmatic
order of people, to whom one may at any time safely communicate a
remarkable piece of news without incurring the danger of having
one's ears pierced by some shrill ejaculation and subsequently stunned
by a torrent of wordy wonderment. Mary did look up, and she did
stare at me; the ladle with which she was basting a pair of chickens
roasting at the fire, did for some three minutes hang suspended in air,
and for the same space of time John's knives also had rest from the
polishing process; but Mary, bending again over the roast, said only --
   'Have you, miss? Well, for sure!'
   A short time after she pursued, 'I seed you go out with the master,
but I didn't know you were gone to church to be wed'; and she
basted away. John, when I turned to him, was grinning from ear to
ear.
   'I telled Mary how it would be,' he said: 'I knew what Mr Ed-
ward' (John was an old servant, and had known his master when he
was the cadet of the house, therefore he often gave him his Christian
name) -- 'I knew what Mr Edward would do; and I was certain he
would not wait long either: and he's done right, for aught I know. I
wish you joy, miss!' and he politely pulled his forelock.
   'Thank you, John. Mr Rochester told me to give you and Mary
this.'
   I put into his hand a five-pound note.  Without waiting to hear
more, I left the kitchen. In passing the door of that sanctum some time
after, I caught the words --
   'She'll happen do better for him nor ony o' t' grand ladies.' And
again, 'If she ben't one o' th' handsomest, she's noan faa\l, and varry
good-natured; and i' his een she's fair beautiful, onybody may see
that.'
   I wrote to Moor House and to Cambridge immediately, to say what
I had done: fully explaining also why I had thus acted. Diana and

                            474

                 JANE EYRE                      475

Mary approved the step unreservedly. Diana announced that she
would just give me time to get over the honeymoon, and then she
would come and see me.
   'She had better not wait till then, Jane,' said Mr Rochester, when I
read her letter to him; 'if she does, she will be too late, for our honey-
moon will shine our life long: its beams will only fade over your
grave or mine.'
   How St John received the news I don't know: he never answered
the letter in which I communicated it: yet six months after he wrote
to me, without, however, mentioning Mr Rochester's name or allud-
ing to my marriage. His letter was then calm, and though very serious,
kind. He has maintained a regular, though not very frequent correspond-
ence ever since: he hopes I am happy, and trusts I am not of those who
live without God in the world, and only mind earthly things.

      </block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">This transcription suffers from a number of shortcomings: <list-block provisional-distance-between-starts="18pt"
                        provisional-label-separation="6pt"
                        text-indent="0em"
                        margin-right="10pt"
                        space-before="6pt"
                        space-after="6pt">
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">the page numbers and running titles are intermingled with the text in a way which makes it difficult for software to distinguish them;</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">no distinction is made between single quotation marks and apostrophe, so it is difficult to be certain exactly which passages are in direct speech;</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">the preservation of the copy text's hyphenation means that simple-minded search programs will not find words broken across a line;</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">the accented letter in <inline font-style="italic" font-weight="normal">faàl</inline> and the long dash have been rendered by ad hoc keying conventions (<inline font-style="italic" font-weight="normal">faa\l</inline>) which follow no standard pattern and will be processed correctly only if the transcriber remembers to mention them in the documentation;</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">paragraph divisions are marked only by the use of white space, and hard carriage returns have been introduced at the end of each line. Consequently, if the size of type used to display the text changes, reformatting will be problematic.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
            </list-block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">We now present the same passage, as it might be encoded in TEI simplePrint. As we shall see, there are many ways in which this encoding could be extended, but as a minimum, the TEI approach allows us to represent the following distinctions in a standardized way: <list-block provisional-distance-between-starts="18pt"
                        provisional-label-separation="6pt"
                        text-indent="0em"
                        margin-right="10pt"
                        space-before="6pt"
                        space-after="6pt">
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">Paragraph and chapter divisions are now marked explicitly by means of tags rather than implicitly by white space.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">Apostrophes are retained, but the quotation marks indicating direct speech have been removed, and direct speech is now marked explicitly by means of a tag. </block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">The accented letter and the long dash are accurately represented, using the appropriate Unicode character.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">Page divisions have been marked with an empty <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.pb" text-decoration="underline">&lt;pb&gt;</basic-link> tag; the page heading and running text have been suppressed.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">The lineation of the original has also been suppressed and words broken by typographic accident at the end of a line have been re-assembled without comment.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">For convenience of proof reading, a new line has been introduced at the start of each paragraph, but the indentation is removed.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
            </list-block>
            <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">
               <inline>&lt;pb n="474"/&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;div type="chapter" n="38"&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;p&gt;</inline>Reader, I married him. A quiet wedding we had: he and I, the parson and clerk, were
   alone present. When we got back from church, I went into the kitchen of the
   manor-house, where Mary was cooking the dinner, and John cleaning the knives, and I
   said —<inline>&lt;/p&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;p&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;q&gt;</inline>Mary, I have been married to Mr Rochester this morning.<inline>&lt;/q&gt;</inline> The housekeeper and
   her husband were of that decent, phlegmatic order of people, to whom one may at any
   time safely communicate a remarkable piece of news without incurring the danger of
   having one's ears pierced by some shrill ejaculation and subsequently stunned by a
   torrent of wordy wonderment. Mary did look up, and she did stare at me; the ladle
   with which she was basting a pair of chickens roasting at the fire, did for some
   three minutes hang suspended in air, and for the same space of time John's knives
   also had rest from the polishing process; but Mary, bending again over the roast,
   said only —<inline>&lt;/p&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;p&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;q&gt;</inline>Have you, miss? Well, for sure!<inline>&lt;/q&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;/p&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;p&gt;</inline>A short time after she pursued, <inline>&lt;q&gt;</inline>I seed you go out with the master, but I didn't
     know you were gone to church to be wed<inline>&lt;/q&gt;</inline>; and she basted away. John, when I
   turned to him, was grinning from ear to ear. <inline>&lt;q&gt;</inline>I telled Mary how it would be,<inline>&lt;/q&gt;</inline>
   he said: <inline>&lt;q&gt;</inline>I knew what Mr Edward<inline>&lt;/q&gt;</inline> (John was an old servant, and had known his
   master when he was the cadet of the house, therefore he often gave him his Christian
   name) — <inline>&lt;q&gt;</inline>I knew what Mr Edward would do; and I was certain he would not wait long
     either: and he's done right, for aught I know. I wish you joy, miss!<inline>&lt;/q&gt;</inline> and he
   politely pulled his forelock.<inline>&lt;/p&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;p&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;q&gt;</inline>Thank you, John. Mr Rochester told me to give you and Mary this.<inline>&lt;/q&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;/p&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;p&gt;</inline>I put into his hand a five-pound note. Without waiting to hear more, I left the
   kitchen. In passing the door of that sanctum some time after, I caught the words
   —<inline>&lt;/p&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;p&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;q&gt;</inline>She'll happen do better for him nor ony o' t' grand ladies.<inline>&lt;/q&gt;</inline> And again, <inline>&lt;q&gt;</inline>If
     she ben't one o' th' handsomest, she's noan faàl, and varry good-natured; and i'
     his een she's fair beautiful, onybody may see that.<inline>&lt;/q&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;/p&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;p&gt;</inline>I wrote to Moor House and to Cambridge immediately, to say what I had done: fully
   explaining also why I had thus acted. Diana and <inline>&lt;pb n="475"/&gt;</inline> Mary approved the step
   unreservedly. Diana announced that she would just give me time to get over the
   honeymoon, and then she would come and see me.<inline>&lt;/p&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;p&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;q&gt;</inline>She had better not wait till then, Jane,<inline>&lt;/q&gt;</inline> said Mr Rochester, when I read her
   letter to him; <inline>&lt;q&gt;</inline>if she does, she will be too late, for our honeymoon will shine
     our life long: its beams will only fade over your grave or mine.<inline>&lt;/q&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;/p&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;p&gt;</inline>How St John received the news I don't know: he never answered the letter in which I
   communicated it: yet six months after he wrote to me, without, however, mentioning
   Mr Rochester's name or alluding to my marriage. His letter was then calm, and though
   very serious, kind. He has maintained a regular, though not very frequent
   correspondence ever since: he hopes I am happy, and trusts I am not of those who
   live without God in the world, and only mind earthly things.<inline>&lt;/p&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;/div&gt;</inline>
            </block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">This encoding is expressed in TEI XML, a very widely used and standardized method of representing information about a document within the document itself. The transcribed words are complemented by special flags within angle brackets, called <inline font-style="italic" font-weight="normal">tags</inline>, which both characterise and mark the beginning and end of a string of characters. For example, each paragraph is marked by a tag <inline>&lt;p&gt;</inline> at its start, and a corresponding <inline>&lt;/p&gt;</inline> at its end. We don't elaborate further on the syntax of TEI XML here. <footnote>
               <inline font-size="7pt" vertical-align="super">1</inline>
               <footnote-body>
                  <block end-indent="0pt"
                         start-indent="0pt"
                         text-align="start"
                         font-style="normal"
                         text-indent="1em"
                         font-size="9pt"
                         line-height="normal">
                     <inline font-size="7pt" vertical-align="super">1</inline> Many introductory tutorials on XML are available on the web, for example at <basic-link external-destination="url(http://www.w3schools.com/xml/)">http://www.w3schools.com/xml/</basic-link>. The way the TEI uses XML is fully documented in <basic-link external-destination="url(http://www.tei-c.org/release/doc/tei-p5-doc/en/html/SG.html)">section v of the TEI Guidelines</basic-link>; a very basic introduction to TEI XML is also available at <basic-link external-destination="url(http://www.ultraslavonic.info/intro-to-xml)">http://www.ultraslavonic.info/intro-to-xml</basic-link>. The formal specification of the XML language is at <basic-link external-destination="url(http://www.w3.org/TR/xml)">http://www.w3.org/TR/xml</basic-link>.</block>
               </footnote-body>
            </footnote>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">Aside from its syntax, it is important to note that this particular encoding represents a set of choices or priorities. We have chosen to prioritize and simplify the representation of the words of the text over the representation of the typographic layout associated with them in this source document. This makes it easier for a computer to answer questions about the words in the document than about its typesetting, reflecting our research priorities. This priority also leads us to suppress end-of-line hyphenation. Conceivably Brontë (or her printer) intended the word ‘honeymoon’ to appear as ‘honey-moon’ on its second appearance, though this seems unlikely: our decision to focus on Brontë's text, rather than on the printing of it in this particular edition, makes it impossible to be certain. Similarly, our decision makes it impossible to use this transcription as a means of statistically analysing hyphenation practice. An encoding makes explicit all and only those textual features of importance to the encoder.</block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">It is not difficult to think of ways in which the encoding of even this short passage might readily be extended to address other research priorities. For example: <list-block provisional-distance-between-starts="18pt"
                        provisional-label-separation="6pt"
                        text-indent="0em"
                        margin-right="10pt"
                        space-before="6pt"
                        space-after="6pt">
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">a regularized form of the passages in dialect could be provided; </block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">footnotes glossing or commenting on any passage could be added;</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">pointers linking parts of this text to others could be added;</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">proper names of various kinds could be distinguished from the surrounding text;</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">names could be classified as personal, geographical, or institutional</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">detailed bibliographic information about the text's provenance and context could be prefixed to it;</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">a linguistic analysis of the passage into sentences, clauses, words, etc., could be provided, each unit being associated with appropriate category codes;</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">the text could be segmented into narrative or discourse units;</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">systematic analysis or interpretation of the text could be included in the encoding, with potentially complex alignment or linkage between the text and the analysis, or between the text and one or more translations of it;</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">passages in the text could be linked to images or sound held on other media.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
            </list-block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">In the remainder of this document, we present a number of TEI-recommended ways of supporting these and other encoding requirements. These ways generally involve the application of specific TEI XML elements, selected from the full range of possibilities documented in the TEI <inline font-style="italic">Guidelines</inline>. Like every other TEI project, TEI Simple proposes a view of the TEI Guidelines. This document defines and documents that view.</block>
         <block keep-with-next.within-page="always"
                id="Simple-struc"
                text-align="left"
                font-family="Times"
                font-size="18pt"
                font-weight="bold"
                space-after="6pt"
                space-before.optimum="12pt"
                text-indent="0em">2. The Structure of a TEI simplePrint Document</block>
         <block line-height="1">A TEI-conformant text contains (a) a <inline font-style="italic" font-weight="normal">TEI header</inline> (marked up as a <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.teiHeader" text-decoration="underline">&lt;teiHeader&gt;</basic-link> element) and (b) one or more representations of a text. These representations may be of three kinds: a transcribed text, marked up as a <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.text" text-decoration="underline">&lt;text&gt;</basic-link> element; a collection of digital images representing the text, marked up using a <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.facsimile" text-decoration="underline">&lt;facsimile&gt;</basic-link> element; or a literal transcription of one or more documents instantiating the text, marked up using the <inline hyphenate="false" color="black" font-family="Courier">&lt;sourceDoc&gt;</inline> element.</block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">These elements are combined together to form a single <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.TEI" text-decoration="underline">&lt;TEI&gt;</basic-link> element, which must be declared within the TEI <inline font-style="italic" font-weight="normal">namespace</inline>, and therefore usually takes the form <inline>&lt;TEI xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0"&gt;</inline>
            <footnote>
               <inline font-size="7pt" vertical-align="super">2</inline>
               <footnote-body>
                  <block end-indent="0pt"
                         start-indent="0pt"
                         text-align="start"
                         font-style="normal"
                         text-indent="1em"
                         font-size="9pt"
                         line-height="normal">
                     <inline font-size="7pt" vertical-align="super">2</inline> A <inline font-style="italic" font-weight="normal">namespace</inline> is an XML concept. Its function is to identify the vocabulary from which a group of element names are drawn, using a standard identifier resembling a web address. The namespace for TEI elements is <inline>http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0</inline>
                  </block>
               </footnote-body>
            </footnote>.</block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">Some aspects of the TEI header are described in more detail in section <basic-link internal-destination="Simple-header" text-decoration="underline">15 (The Electronic Title Page)</basic-link>. In what follows, we will focus chiefly on the use of the <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.text" text-decoration="underline">&lt;text&gt;</basic-link> element, though we describe one way of using the <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.facsimile" text-decoration="underline">&lt;facsimile&gt;</basic-link> element in combination with it or alone in section <basic-link internal-destination="Simple-fax" text-decoration="underline">14 (Encoding a Digital Facsimile)</basic-link>. We do not consider the <inline hyphenate="false" color="black" font-family="Courier">&lt;sourceDoc&gt;</inline> element further, since it is mainly used in very specialised applications for which TEI simplePrint would not be appropriate.</block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">A text may be <inline font-style="italic" font-weight="normal">unitary</inline> (a single work) or <inline font-style="italic" font-weight="normal">composite</inline> (a collection of single works, such as an anthology). In either case, the text may have optional <inline font-style="italic" font-weight="normal">front</inline> or <inline font-style="italic" font-weight="normal">back</inline> matter such as title pages, prefaces, appendixes etc. We use the term <inline font-style="italic" font-weight="normal">body</inline> for whatever comes between these in the source document. We discuss various kinds of composite text in section <basic-link internal-destination="Simple-composite" text-decoration="underline">12 (Composite and Floating Texts)</basic-link> below.</block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">A unitary text will be encoded using an overall structure like this: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">
               <inline>&lt;TEI xmlns="http://www.tei-c.org/ns/1.0"&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;teiHeader&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;!-- [ TEI Header information ]  --&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;/teiHeader&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;text&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;front&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;!-- [ front matter ... ] --&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;/front&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;body&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;!-- [ body of text ... ]  --&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;/body&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;back&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;!--  [ back matter ...  ] --&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;/back&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;/text&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;/TEI&gt;</inline>
            </block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">In each of the following sections we include a short list of the TEI <inline font-style="italic" font-weight="normal">elements</inline> under discussion, along with a brief description, and in most cases an example of how they are used. Throughout the text, element names are linked to their detailed reference documentation, as given in the TEI Guidelines. Note that most of the examples provided by the reference documentation, and all of the links, are not specific to TEI simplePrint.</block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">For example, here are the elements discussed so far: <list-block provisional-distance-between-starts="18pt"
                        provisional-label-separation="6pt"
                        text-indent="0em"
                        margin-right="10pt"
                        space-before="6pt"
                        space-after="6pt">
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.TEI" text-decoration="underline">TEI</basic-link>
                        </inline> (TEI document) contains a single TEI-conformant document, combining a single TEI header with one or more members of the <inline color="black" font-family="Helvetica">model.resourceLike</inline> class. Multiple TEI elements may be combined to form a <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.teiCorpus" text-decoration="underline">&lt;teiCorpus&gt;</basic-link> element.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.teiHeader" text-decoration="underline">teiHeader</basic-link>
                        </inline> (TEI header) supplies descriptive and declarative metadata associated with a digital resource or set of resources.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.text" text-decoration="underline">text</basic-link>
                        </inline> contains a single text of any kind, whether unitary or composite, for example a poem or drama, a collection of essays, a novel, a dictionary, or a corpus sample.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.facsimile" text-decoration="underline">facsimile</basic-link>
                        </inline> contains a representation of some written source in the form of a set of images rather than as transcribed or encoded text.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
            </list-block>
         </block>
         <block keep-with-next.within-page="always"
                id="Simple-body"
                text-align="left"
                font-family="Times"
                font-size="18pt"
                font-weight="bold"
                space-after="6pt"
                space-before.optimum="12pt"
                text-indent="0em">3. Encoding the Body</block>
         <block line-height="1">As indicated above, a unitary text is encoded by means of a <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.text" text-decoration="underline">&lt;text&gt;</basic-link> element, which may contain the following elements: <list-block provisional-distance-between-starts="18pt"
                        provisional-label-separation="6pt"
                        text-indent="0em"
                        margin-right="10pt"
                        space-before="6pt"
                        space-after="6pt">
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.front" text-decoration="underline">front</basic-link>
                        </inline> (front matter) contains any prefatory matter (headers, abstracts, title page, prefaces, dedications, etc.) found at the start of a document, before the main body.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.group" text-decoration="underline">group</basic-link>
                        </inline> contains the body of a composite text, grouping together a sequence of distinct texts (or groups of such texts) which are regarded as a unit for some purpose, for example the collected works of an author, a sequence of prose essays, etc.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.body" text-decoration="underline">body</basic-link>
                        </inline> (text body) contains the whole body of a single unitary text, excluding any front or back matter.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.back" text-decoration="underline">back</basic-link>
                        </inline> (back matter) contains any appendixes, etc. following the main part of a text.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
            </list-block> Elements specific to front and back matter are described below in section <basic-link internal-destination="Simple-fronbac" text-decoration="underline">13 (Front and Back Matter)</basic-link>. In this section we discuss the elements making up the body of a text. A text must always have a body.</block>
         <block keep-with-next.within-page="always"
                id="divs"
                text-align="left"
                font-family="Times"
                font-size="14pt"
                font-weight="bold"
                space-after="3pt"
                space-before.optimum="9pt"
                text-indent="0em">3.1. Text Division Elements and Global Attributes</block>
         <block line-height="1">The body of a prose text may be just a series of paragraphs or similar blocks of text, or these may be grouped together into chapters, sections, subsections, etc. The <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.div" text-decoration="underline">&lt;div&gt;</basic-link> element is used to represent any such grouping of blocks. <list-block provisional-distance-between-starts="18pt"
                        provisional-label-separation="6pt"
                        text-indent="0em"
                        margin-right="10pt"
                        space-before="6pt"
                        space-after="6pt">
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.div" text-decoration="underline">div</basic-link>
                        </inline> (text division) contains a subdivision of the front, body, or back of a text.<table text-align="left"
                               font-size="9pt"
                               table-layout="fixed"
                               width="100%"
                               id="d27e482">
                           <table-column column-number="1" column-width="14.285714285714285%"/>
                           <table-column column-number="2" column-width="85.71428571428571%"/>
                           <table-body text-indent="0pt">
                              <table-row>
                                 <table-cell padding="2pt" text-align="left">
                                    <block>
                                       <inline font-style="italic">type [att.typed]</inline>
                                    </block>
                                 </table-cell>
                                 <table-cell padding="2pt" text-align="left">
                                    <block>characterizes the element in some sense, using any convenient classification scheme or typology.</block>
                                 </table-cell>
                              </table-row>
                           </table-body>
                        </table>
                     </block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
            </list-block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">The <inline font-style="italic">type</inline> attribute on the <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.div" text-decoration="underline">&lt;div&gt;</basic-link> element may be used to supply a conventional name for this category of text division in order to distinguish them. Typical values might be <inline>book</inline>, <inline>chapter</inline>, <inline>section</inline>, <inline>part</inline>, <inline>poem</inline>, <inline>song</inline>, etc. TEI simplePrint does not constrain the range of values that may be used here.</block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">A <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.div" text-decoration="underline">&lt;div&gt;</basic-link> element may itself contain further, nested, <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.div" text-decoration="underline">&lt;div&gt;</basic-link>s, thus mimicking the traditional structure of a book, which can be decomposed hierarchically into units such as parts, containing chapters, containing sections, and so on. TEI texts in general conform to this simple hierarchic model.</block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">Here as elsewhere the <inline font-style="italic">xml:id</inline> attribute may be used to supply a unique identifier for the division, which may be used for cross references or other links to it, such as a commentary, as further discussed in section <basic-link internal-destination="Simple-ptrs" text-decoration="underline">3.7 (Cross References and Links)</basic-link>. It is good practice to provide an <inline font-style="italic">xml:id</inline> attribute for every major structural unit in a text, and to derive its values in some systematic way, for example by appending a section number to a short code for the title of the work in question, as in the examples below.</block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">The <inline font-style="italic">n</inline> attribute may be used to supply (additionally or alternatively) a short mnemonic name or number for a division, or any other element. If a conventional form of reference or abbreviation for the parts of a work already exists (such as the book/chapter/verse pattern of Biblical citations), the <inline font-style="italic">n</inline> attribute is the place to record it; unlike the identifier supplied by the <inline font-style="italic">xml:id</inline> attribute, it does not need to be unique.</block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">The <inline font-style="italic">xml:lang</inline> attribute may be used to specify the language of the division. Languages are identified by an internationally defined code, as further discussed in section <basic-link internal-destination="z636" text-decoration="underline">3.5.3 (Foreign Words or Expressions)</basic-link> below.</block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">The <inline font-style="italic">rendition</inline> attribute may be used to supply information about the rendition (appearance) of a division, or any other element, as further discussed in section <basic-link internal-destination="Simple-hilites" text-decoration="underline">3.5 (Marking Highlighted Phrases)</basic-link> below. Note that this attribute is used to describe the appearance of the <inline font-style="italic">source</inline> text, rather than the appearance of any intended output when the encoded text is displayed. The two may of course be similar, or identical, but the TEI does not assume or require this.</block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">These four attributes, <inline font-style="italic">xml:id</inline>, <inline font-style="italic">n</inline>, <inline font-style="italic">xml:lang</inline>, and <inline font-style="italic">rendition</inline> are so widely useful that they are allowed on any element in any TEI schema: they are called <inline font-style="italic" font-weight="normal">global attributes</inline>. Other attributes defined in the TEI simplePrint schema are discussed in section <basic-link internal-destination="xatts" text-decoration="underline">3.7.3 (Special Kinds of Linking)</basic-link>.</block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">As noted above, the value of every <inline font-style="italic">xml:id</inline> attribute must be unique within a document. One simple way of ensuring this is to make it reflect the hierarchic structure of the document. For example, Smith's <inline font-style="italic">Wealth of Nations</inline> as first published consists of five books, each of which is divided into chapters, while some chapters are further subdivided into parts. We might define <inline font-style="italic">xml:id</inline> values for this structure as follows: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">
               <inline>&lt;body&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;div xml:id="WN1" n="I" type="book"&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;div xml:id="WN101" n="I.1" type="chapter"&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;!-- ... --&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;/div&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;div xml:id="WN102" n="I.2" type="chapter"&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;!-- ... --&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;/div&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;!-- ... --&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;div xml:id="WN110" n="I.10"
   type="chapter"&gt;</inline>
   <inline>&lt;div xml:id="WN1101" n="I.10.1"
    type="part"&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;!-- ... --&gt;</inline>
   <inline>&lt;/div&gt;</inline>
   <inline>&lt;div xml:id="WN1102" n="I.10.2"
    type="part"&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;!-- ... --&gt;</inline>
   <inline>&lt;/div&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;/div&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;!-- ... --&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;/div&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;div xml:id="WN2" n="II" type="book"&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;!-- ... --&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;/div&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;/body&gt;</inline>
            </block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">A different numbering scheme may be used for <inline font-style="italic">xml:id</inline> and <inline font-style="italic">n</inline> attributes: this is often useful where a canonical reference scheme is used which does not tally with the structure of the work. For example, in a novel divided into books each containing chapters, where the chapters are numbered sequentially through the whole work, rather than within each book, one might use a scheme such as the following: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">
               <inline>&lt;body&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;div xml:id="TS01" n="1" type="volume"&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;div xml:id="TS011" n="1" type="chapter"&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;!-- ... --&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;/div&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;div xml:id="TS012" n="2" type="chapter"&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;!-- ... --&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;/div&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;/div&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;div xml:id="TS02" n="2" type="volume"&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;div xml:id="TS021" n="3" type="chapter"&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;!-- ... --&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;/div&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;div xml:id="TS022" n="4" type="chapter"&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;!-- ... --&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;/div&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;/div&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;/body&gt;</inline>
            </block> Here the work has two volumes, each containing two chapters. The chapters are numbered conventionally 1 to 4, but the <inline font-style="italic">xml:id</inline> values specified allow them to be regarded additionally as if they were numbered 1.1, 1.2, 2.1, 2.2.</block>
         <block keep-with-next.within-page="always"
                id="h25"
                text-align="left"
                font-family="Times"
                font-size="14pt"
                font-weight="bold"
                space-after="3pt"
                space-before.optimum="9pt"
                text-indent="0em">3.2. Headings and Closings</block>
         <block line-height="1">Every <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.div" text-decoration="underline">&lt;div&gt;</basic-link> may have a title or heading at its start, and (less commonly) a trailer such as ‘End of Chapter 1’ at its end. The following elements may be used to transcribe them: <list-block provisional-distance-between-starts="18pt"
                        provisional-label-separation="6pt"
                        text-indent="0em"
                        margin-right="10pt"
                        space-before="6pt"
                        space-after="6pt">
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.head" text-decoration="underline">head</basic-link>
                        </inline> (heading) contains any type of heading, for example the title of a section, or the heading of a list, glossary, manuscript description, etc.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.trailer" text-decoration="underline">trailer</basic-link>
                        </inline> contains a closing title or footer appearing at the end of a division of a text.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
            </list-block> Some other elements which may be found at the beginning or ending of text divisions are discussed below in section <basic-link internal-destination="h52" text-decoration="underline">13.1.2 (Prefatory Matter)</basic-link>.</block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">Whether or not headings and trailers are included in a transcription is a matter for the individual transcriber to decide. Where a heading is completely regular (for example ‘Chapter 1’) or may be automatically constructed from attribute values (e.g. <inline>&lt;div type="chapter" n="1"&gt;</inline>), it may be omitted; where it contains otherwise unrecoverable text it should always be included. For example, the start of Hardy's <inline font-style="italic">Under the Greenwood Tree</inline> might be encoded as follows: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">
               <inline>&lt;div xml:id="UGT1" n="Winter" type="part"&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;div xml:id="UGT101" n="1" type="chapter"&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;head&gt;</inline>Mellstock-Lane<inline>&lt;/head&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;p&gt;</inline>To dwellers in a wood almost every species of tree ... <inline>&lt;/p&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;/div&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;/div&gt;</inline>
            </block>
         </block>
         <block keep-with-next.within-page="always"
                id="vedr"
                text-align="left"
                font-family="Times"
                font-size="14pt"
                font-weight="bold"
                space-after="3pt"
                space-before.optimum="9pt"
                text-indent="0em">3.3. Textual Components</block>
         <block line-height="1">In prose texts such as the Brontë example above, the divisions are generally composed of paragraphs, represented as <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.p" text-decoration="underline">&lt;p&gt;</basic-link> elements, though in some circumstances it may be preferred to use the ‘anonymous block’ element <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.ab" text-decoration="underline">&lt;ab&gt;</basic-link>. In poetic or dramatic texts different elements are used, representing stanzas and verse lines in the first case, and individual speeches or stage directions in the second: <list-block provisional-distance-between-starts="18pt"
                        provisional-label-separation="6pt"
                        text-indent="0em"
                        margin-right="10pt"
                        space-before="6pt"
                        space-after="6pt">
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.p" text-decoration="underline">p</basic-link>
                        </inline> (paragraph) marks paragraphs in prose.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.ab" text-decoration="underline">ab</basic-link>
                        </inline> (anonymous block) contains any arbitrary component-level unit of text, acting as an anonymous container for phrase or inter level elements analogous to, but without the semantic baggage of, a paragraph.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.l" text-decoration="underline">l</basic-link>
                        </inline> (verse line) contains a single, possibly incomplete, line of verse.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.lg" text-decoration="underline">lg</basic-link>
                        </inline> (line group) contains one or more verse lines functioning as a formal unit, e.g. a stanza, refrain, verse paragraph, etc.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.sp" text-decoration="underline">sp</basic-link>
                        </inline> (speech) contains an individual speech in a performance text, or a passage presented as such in a prose or verse text.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.speaker" text-decoration="underline">speaker</basic-link>
                        </inline> contains a specialized form of heading or label, giving the name of one or more speakers in a dramatic text or fragment.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.stage" text-decoration="underline">stage</basic-link>
                        </inline> (stage direction) contains any kind of stage direction within a dramatic text or fragment.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
            </list-block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">We discuss each of these kinds of component separately below.</block>
         <block keep-with-next.within-page="always"
                id="d27e772"
                text-align="left"
                font-family="Times"
                font-size="12pt"
                font-weight="bold"
                font-style="italic"
                space-after="2pt"
                space-before.optimum="4pt"
                text-indent="0em">3.3.1. Verse</block>
         <block line-height="1">Here, for example, is the start of a poetic text in which verse lines and stanzas are tagged: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">
               <inline>&lt;lg n="I"&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;l&gt;</inline>I Sing the progresse of a deathlesse soule,<inline>&lt;/l&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;l&gt;</inline>Whom Fate, with God made, but doth not controule,<inline>&lt;/l&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;!-- ... --&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;l&gt;</inline>A worke t'out weare Seths pillars, bricke and stone,<inline>&lt;/l&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;l&gt;</inline>And (holy writs excepted) made to yeeld to none,<inline>&lt;/l&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;/lg&gt;</inline>
            </block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">Note that the <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.l" text-decoration="underline">&lt;l&gt;</basic-link> element marks verse lines, not typographic lines: as elsewhere the original lineation of the source text is not therefore preserved by this encoding. The <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.lb" text-decoration="underline">&lt;lb&gt;</basic-link> element described in section <basic-link internal-destination="Simple-pln" text-decoration="underline">3.4 (Page and Line Numbers)</basic-link> might additionally be used to mark typographic lines if so desired.</block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">In a poetic text it may also be considered useful to identify the rhymes, for which the following element may be used: <list-block provisional-distance-between-starts="18pt"
                        provisional-label-separation="6pt"
                        text-indent="0em"
                        margin-right="10pt"
                        space-before="6pt"
                        space-after="6pt">
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.rhyme" text-decoration="underline">rhyme</basic-link>
                        </inline> marks the rhyming part of a metrical line.<table text-align="left"
                               font-size="9pt"
                               table-layout="fixed"
                               width="100%"
                               id="d27e804">
                           <table-body text-indent="0pt">
                              <table-row>
                                 <table-cell padding="2pt" text-align="left">
                                    <block>
                                       <inline font-style="italic">label</inline>
                                    </block>
                                 </table-cell>
                                 <table-cell padding="2pt" text-align="left">
                                    <block>provides a label (usually a single letter) to identify which part of a rhyme scheme this rhyming string instantiates.</block>
                                 </table-cell>
                              </table-row>
                           </table-body>
                        </table>
                     </block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
            </list-block> The following example shows how this element might be used both to identify rhyming words or word parts and to assign each rhyme to a part of a rhyming pattern by means of its <inline font-style="italic">label</inline> attribute. The rhyming pattern here is specified by the <inline font-style="italic">rhyme</inline> attribute supplied on the <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.lg" text-decoration="underline">&lt;lg&gt;</basic-link> representing the stanza within which the pattern operates: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">
               <inline>&lt;lg rhyme="AABCCBBA"&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;l&gt;</inline>The sunlight on the <inline>&lt;rhyme label="A"&gt;</inline>garden<inline>&lt;/rhyme&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;/l&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;l&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;rhyme label="A"&gt;</inline>Harden<inline>&lt;/rhyme&gt;</inline>s and grows <inline>&lt;rhyme label="B"&gt;</inline>cold<inline>&lt;/rhyme&gt;</inline>,<inline>&lt;/l&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;l&gt;</inline>We cannot cage the <inline>&lt;rhyme label="C"&gt;</inline>minute<inline>&lt;/rhyme&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;/l&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;l&gt;</inline>Wi<inline>&lt;rhyme label="C"&gt;</inline>thin it<inline>&lt;/rhyme&gt;</inline>s nets of <inline>&lt;rhyme label="B"&gt;</inline>gold<inline>&lt;/rhyme&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;/l&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;l&gt;</inline>When all is <inline>&lt;rhyme label="B"&gt;</inline>told<inline>&lt;/rhyme&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;/l&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;l&gt;</inline>We cannot beg for <inline>&lt;rhyme label="A"&gt;</inline>pardon<inline>&lt;/rhyme&gt;</inline>.<inline>&lt;/l&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;/lg&gt;</inline>
            </block> The <inline font-style="italic">rhyme</inline> attribute may be used independently of the <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.rhyme" text-decoration="underline">&lt;rhyme&gt;</basic-link> element, or in combination with it, as above.</block>
         <block keep-with-next.within-page="always"
                id="d27e863"
                text-align="left"
                font-family="Times"
                font-size="12pt"
                font-weight="bold"
                font-style="italic"
                space-after="2pt"
                space-before.optimum="4pt"
                text-indent="0em">3.3.2. Drama</block>
         <block line-height="1">A dramatic text contains speeches, which may be in prose or verse, and will also contain stage directions. The <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.sp" text-decoration="underline">&lt;sp&gt;</basic-link> element is used to represent each identified speech. It contains an optional speaker indication, marked with the <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.speaker" text-decoration="underline">&lt;speaker&gt;</basic-link> element, which can be followed by one or more <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.l" text-decoration="underline">&lt;l&gt;</basic-link> or <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.p" text-decoration="underline">&lt;p&gt;</basic-link> elements, depending on whether the speech is considered to be in prose or in verse. Stage directions, whether within or between speeches, are marked using the <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.stage" text-decoration="underline">&lt;stage&gt;</basic-link> element.</block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">For example: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">
               <inline>&lt;sp&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;speaker&gt;</inline>Vladimir<inline>&lt;/speaker&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;p&gt;</inline>Pull on your trousers.<inline>&lt;/p&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;/sp&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;sp&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;speaker&gt;</inline>Estragon<inline>&lt;/speaker&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;p&gt;</inline>You want me to pull off my trousers?<inline>&lt;/p&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;/sp&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;sp&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;speaker&gt;</inline>Vladimir<inline>&lt;/speaker&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;p&gt;</inline>Pull <inline>&lt;hi&gt;</inline>on<inline>&lt;/hi&gt;</inline> your trousers.<inline>&lt;/p&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;/sp&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;sp&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;speaker&gt;</inline>Vladimir<inline>&lt;/speaker&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;p&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;stage&gt;</inline>(realizing his trousers are down)<inline>&lt;/stage&gt;</inline>. True<inline>&lt;/p&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;/sp&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;stage&gt;</inline>He pulls up his trousers<inline>&lt;/stage&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;sp&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;speaker&gt;</inline>Vladimir<inline>&lt;/speaker&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;p&gt;</inline>Well? Shall we go?<inline>&lt;/p&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;/sp&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;sp&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;speaker&gt;</inline>Estragon<inline>&lt;/speaker&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;p&gt;</inline>Yes, let's go.<inline>&lt;/p&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;/sp&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;stage&gt;</inline>They do not move.<inline>&lt;/stage&gt;</inline>
            </block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">In a verse drama, it is quite common to find that verse lines are split between speakers. The easiest way of encoding this is to use the <inline font-style="italic">part</inline> attribute to indicate that the lines so fragmented are incomplete: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">
               <inline>&lt;div type="Act" n="I"&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;head&gt;</inline>ACT I<inline>&lt;/head&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;div type="Scene" n="1"&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;head&gt;</inline>SCENE I<inline>&lt;/head&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;stage rendition="#italic"&gt;</inline>Enter Barnardo and Francisco, two Sentinels, at
     several doors<inline>&lt;/stage&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;sp&gt;</inline>
   <inline>&lt;speaker&gt;</inline>Barn<inline>&lt;/speaker&gt;</inline>
   <inline>&lt;l part="Y"&gt;</inline>Who's there?<inline>&lt;/l&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;/sp&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;sp&gt;</inline>
   <inline>&lt;speaker&gt;</inline>Fran<inline>&lt;/speaker&gt;</inline>
   <inline>&lt;l&gt;</inline>Nay, answer me. Stand and unfold yourself.<inline>&lt;/l&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;/sp&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;sp&gt;</inline>
   <inline>&lt;speaker&gt;</inline>Barn<inline>&lt;/speaker&gt;</inline>
   <inline>&lt;l part="I"&gt;</inline>Long live the King!<inline>&lt;/l&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;/sp&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;sp&gt;</inline>
   <inline>&lt;speaker&gt;</inline>Fran<inline>&lt;/speaker&gt;</inline>
   <inline>&lt;l part="M"&gt;</inline>Barnardo?<inline>&lt;/l&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;/sp&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;sp&gt;</inline>
   <inline>&lt;speaker&gt;</inline>Barn<inline>&lt;/speaker&gt;</inline>
   <inline>&lt;l part="F"&gt;</inline>He.<inline>&lt;/l&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;/sp&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;sp&gt;</inline>
   <inline>&lt;speaker&gt;</inline>Fran<inline>&lt;/speaker&gt;</inline>
   <inline>&lt;l&gt;</inline>You come most carefully upon your hour.<inline>&lt;/l&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;/sp&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;!-- ... --&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;/div&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;/div&gt;</inline>
            </block> The value of the <inline font-style="italic">part</inline> attribute may indicate just that the element bearing is fragmented in some (unspecified) respect rather than a complete verse line (<inline font-family="Courier">part="Y"</inline>); alternatively it may indicate whether this is an initial (I), medial (M) or F (final) fragment.</block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">The same mechanism may be applied to stanzas which are divided between two speakers: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">
               <inline>&lt;div&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;sp&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;speaker&gt;</inline>First voice<inline>&lt;/speaker&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;lg type="stanza" part="I"&gt;</inline>
   <inline>&lt;l&gt;</inline>But why drives on that ship so fast<inline>&lt;/l&gt;</inline>
   <inline>&lt;l&gt;</inline>Withouten wave or wind?<inline>&lt;/l&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;/lg&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;/sp&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;sp&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;speaker&gt;</inline>Second Voice<inline>&lt;/speaker&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;lg type="stanza" part="F"&gt;</inline>
   <inline>&lt;l&gt;</inline>The air is cut away before.<inline>&lt;/l&gt;</inline>
   <inline>&lt;l&gt;</inline>And closes from behind.<inline>&lt;/l&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;/lg&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;/sp&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;!-- ... --&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;/div&gt;</inline>
            </block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">The <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.sp" text-decoration="underline">&lt;sp&gt;</basic-link> element can also be used for dialogue presented in a prose work as if it were drama, as in the next example, which also demonstrates the use of the <inline font-style="italic">who</inline> attribute to bear a code identifying the speaker of the piece of dialogue concerned: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">
               <inline>&lt;div&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;sp who="#OPI"&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;speaker&gt;</inline>The reverend Doctor Opimian<inline>&lt;/speaker&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;p&gt;</inline>I do not think I have named a single unpresentable fish.<inline>&lt;/p&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;/sp&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;sp who="#GRM"&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;speaker&gt;</inline>Mr Gryll<inline>&lt;/speaker&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;p&gt;</inline>Bream, Doctor: there is not much to be said for bream.<inline>&lt;/p&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;/sp&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;sp who="#OPI"&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;speaker&gt;</inline>The Reverend Doctor Opimian<inline>&lt;/speaker&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;p&gt;</inline>On the contrary, sir, I think there is much to be said for him. In the first
     place....<inline>&lt;/p&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;p&gt;</inline>Fish, Miss Gryll -- I could discourse to you on fish by the hour: but for the
     present I will forbear.<inline>&lt;/p&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;/sp&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;/div&gt;</inline>
            </block> Here the <inline font-style="italic">who</inline> attribute values (<inline>#OPI</inline> etc.) are links, pointing to items in a list of the characters in the novel. In the case of a play, this list of characters might appear in the original source as a cast list or dramatic personae, which might be marked up using the <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.castList" text-decoration="underline">&lt;castList&gt;</basic-link> element described in section <basic-link internal-destination="specfronbac" text-decoration="underline">13.2.2 (Specialized Front and Back Matter)</basic-link> below. Such a list would not, of course, be appropriate to provide descriptive information about each character, much of which does not appear in the original source. Instead a <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.particDesc" text-decoration="underline">&lt;particDesc&gt;</basic-link> (participant description) element should be provided in the TEI header, as further discussed in section <basic-link internal-destination="profDesc" text-decoration="underline">15.3 (The Profile Description)</basic-link> below.</block>
         <block keep-with-next.within-page="always"
                id="abseg"
                text-align="left"
                font-family="Times"
                font-size="12pt"
                font-weight="bold"
                font-style="italic"
                space-after="2pt"
                space-before.optimum="4pt"
                text-indent="0em">3.3.3. Other Kinds of Text Block</block>
         <block line-height="1">As mentioned above, the <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.ab" text-decoration="underline">&lt;ab&gt;</basic-link> element may also be used in preference to the <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.p" text-decoration="underline">&lt;p&gt;</basic-link> element. It should be used for blocks of text which are not clearly paragraphs, verse lines, or dramatic speeches. Typical examples include the canonical verses of the Bible, and the textual blocks of other ancient documents which predate the invention of the paragraph, such as Greek inscriptions or Egyptian hieroglyphs. The element is also useful as a means of encoding more specialized kinds of textual block, such as the question and answer structure of a catechism, or the highly formalized substructure of a legal document (if <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.div" text-decoration="underline">&lt;div&gt;</basic-link> is not considered appropriate for these). In more modern documents, it can be used to encode semi-organized or fragmentary materials such as an artist's notebook or work in progress; or to faithfully capture the substructure of a file produced by an OCR system.</block>
         <block keep-with-next.within-page="always"
                id="Simple-pln"
                text-align="left"
                font-family="Times"
                font-size="14pt"
                font-weight="bold"
                space-after="3pt"
                space-before.optimum="9pt"
                text-indent="0em">3.4. Page and Line Numbers</block>
         <block line-height="1">Page and line breaks etc. may be marked with the following elements:<list-block provisional-distance-between-starts="18pt"
                        provisional-label-separation="6pt"
                        text-indent="0em"
                        margin-right="10pt"
                        space-before="6pt"
                        space-after="6pt">
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.pb" text-decoration="underline">pb</basic-link>
                        </inline> (page break) marks the start of a new page in a paginated document.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.lb" text-decoration="underline">lb</basic-link>
                        </inline> (line break) marks the start of a new (typographic) line in some edition or version of a text.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.cb" text-decoration="underline">cb</basic-link>
                        </inline> (column break) marks the beginning of a new column of a text on a multi-column page.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.milestone" text-decoration="underline">milestone</basic-link>
                        </inline> marks a boundary point separating any kind of section of a text, typically but not necessarily indicating a point at which some part of a standard reference system changes, where the change is not represented by a structural element.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.fw" text-decoration="underline">fw</basic-link>
                        </inline> (forme work) contains a running head (e.g. a header, footer), catchword, or similar material appearing on the current page.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
            </list-block> The <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.pb" text-decoration="underline">&lt;pb&gt;</basic-link>, <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.lb" text-decoration="underline">&lt;lb&gt;</basic-link>, and <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.cb" text-decoration="underline">&lt;cb&gt;</basic-link> elements are special cases of a general class of elements known as <inline font-style="italic" font-weight="normal">milestone</inline>s because they mark reference points within a text. The generic <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.milestone" text-decoration="underline">&lt;milestone&gt;</basic-link> element can mark any kind of reference point: for example, a column break, the start of a new kind of section not otherwise tagged, a change of author or style, or in general any significant change in the text not enclosed by an XML element. Unlike other elements, milestone elements do not enclose a piece of text and make an assertion about it; instead they indicate a point in the text where something changes, as indicated by a change in the values of the milestone's attributes <inline font-style="italic">unit</inline>, which indicates the ‘something’ concerned, and <inline font-style="italic">n</inline> which indicates the new value.</block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">The <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.pb" text-decoration="underline">&lt;pb&gt;</basic-link>, <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.lb" text-decoration="underline">&lt;lb&gt;</basic-link>, and <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.cb" text-decoration="underline">&lt;cb&gt;</basic-link> elements are shortcuts or <inline font-style="italic" font-weight="normal">syntactic sugar</inline> for <inline>&lt;milestone unit="page"/&gt;</inline>
            <inline>&lt;milestone unit="line"/&gt;</inline> and <inline>&lt;milestone unit="column"/&gt;</inline> respectively.</block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">When working from a paginated original, it is often useful to record its pagination, whether to simplify later proof-reading, or to align the transcribed text with a set of page images, as further discussed below.</block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">Because <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.pb" text-decoration="underline">&lt;pb&gt;</basic-link> and other milestone elements are empty, they may be placed freely within or between other elements. However, it is recommended practice always to put them at the <inline font-style="italic">beginning</inline> of whatever unit it is that their presence implies, and not to nest them within elements contained by that unit. For example, in the following example a page break occurs between two lines of a poem: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">
               <inline>&lt;l&gt;</inline>Mary had a little lamb<inline>&lt;/l&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;pb n="13"/&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;l&gt;</inline>Its fleece was white as snow<inline>&lt;/l&gt;</inline>
            </block> The <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.pb" text-decoration="underline">&lt;pb&gt;</basic-link> element should be placed ahead of all the text encoded on the 13th page. Contrast this with the following less accurate encoding: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">
               <inline>&lt;l&gt;</inline>Mary had a little lamb<inline>&lt;/l&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;l&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;pb n="13"/&gt;</inline>Its fleece was white as snow
<inline>&lt;/l&gt;</inline>
            </block> This is less accurate because it implies that the second verse line actually begins before the page break.</block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">Similar considerations apply to line breaks (<basic-link internal-destination="TEI.lb" text-decoration="underline">&lt;lb&gt;</basic-link>), though these are less frequently considered useful when encoding modern printed textual sources. When transcribing manuscripts or early printed books, however, it is often helpful to retain them in an encoding, if only to facilitate alignment of transcription and original. Like <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.pb" text-decoration="underline">&lt;pb&gt;</basic-link>, the <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.lb" text-decoration="underline">&lt;lb&gt;</basic-link> element should appear <inline font-style="italic">before</inline> the text of the line whose start it signals.</block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">If features such as pagination or lineation are marked for more than one edition, the edition in question may be specified by the <inline font-style="italic">ed</inline> attribute. For example, in the following passage we indicate where the page breaks occur in two different editions (<inline>ED1</inline> and <inline>ED2</inline>): <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">
               <inline>&lt;p&gt;</inline>I wrote to Moor House and to Cambridge immediately, to say what I had done:
 fully explaining also why I had thus acted. Diana and <inline>&lt;pb ed="ED1" n="475"/&gt;</inline> Mary
 approved the step unreservedly. Diana announced that she would <inline>&lt;pb ed="ED2" n="485"/&gt;</inline>just give me time to get over the honeymoon, and then she would come and see
 me.<inline>&lt;/p&gt;</inline>
            </block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">When transcribing from a paginated source, the encoder must decide whether to suppress such features as running titles, page signatures, catch words etc., to replace them by a simplified representation using the <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.pb" text-decoration="underline">&lt;pb&gt;</basic-link> element, perhaps using the <inline font-style="italic">n</inline> attribute to preserve some of the information, or to preserve them entirely using the <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.fw" text-decoration="underline">&lt;fw&gt;</basic-link> element. The latter strategy is appropriate in encodings which aim to retain as much information as possible about the original typography; it will however make more complex the processing of the source for other purposes, as in the following example: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">
               <inline>&lt;l&gt;</inline>He also fix'd the wandering
 QUEEN OF NIGHT,<inline>&lt;/l&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;fw type="sig"&gt;</inline>Ii 2<inline>&lt;/fw&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;fw type="catch"&gt;</inline>Whether<inline>&lt;/fw&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;pb n="244"/&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;l&gt;</inline>Whether she wanes into a scanty orb<inline>&lt;/l&gt;</inline>...
<inline>&lt;!-- Thomson, Seasons, 1730--&gt;</inline>
            </block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">The <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.pb" text-decoration="underline">&lt;pb&gt;</basic-link> element is also used to align parts of a transcription with a digital image of the page concerned. This may be done in a very simple but inflexible way by using the <inline font-style="italic">facs</inline> attribute to point to each page image concerned: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">
               <inline>&lt;p&gt;</inline>I wrote to Moor House and to Cambridge
 immediately, to say what I had done: fully explaining also why I had thus acted.
 Diana and <inline>&lt;pb ed="ED1" n="475" facs="ed1p475.png"/&gt;</inline> Mary approved the step
 unreservedly... <inline>&lt;/p&gt;</inline>
            </block> The <inline font-style="italic">facs</inline> attribute can supply (as here) a filename, or any other form of URI, if for example the page image is stored remotely. One drawback of this simplistic approach is that there must be exactly one image file per page of text. It is not therefore suitable in the case where the available page images represent double page spreads, or where there are multiple images of the same page (for example at different resolutions).</block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">A more powerful approach, discussed in section <basic-link internal-destination="Simple-fax" text-decoration="underline">14 (Encoding a Digital Facsimile)</basic-link> below, is to use the <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.facsimile" text-decoration="underline">&lt;facsimile&gt;</basic-link> element to define the organisation of the set of images representing the text, and then use the <inline font-style="italic">facs</inline> attribute to point to individual components of that representation.</block>
         <block keep-with-next.within-page="always"
                id="Simple-hilites"
                text-align="left"
                font-family="Times"
                font-size="14pt"
                font-weight="bold"
                space-after="3pt"
                space-before.optimum="9pt"
                text-indent="0em">3.5. Marking Highlighted Phrases</block>
         <block keep-with-next.within-page="always"
                id="faces"
                text-align="left"
                font-family="Times"
                font-size="12pt"
                font-weight="bold"
                font-style="italic"
                space-after="2pt"
                space-before.optimum="4pt"
                text-indent="0em">3.5.1. Changes of Typeface, etc.</block>
         <block line-height="1">Highlighted words or phrases are those made visibly different from the rest of the text, typically by a change of type font, handwriting style, ink colour etc., which is intended to draw the reader's attention to some associated change.</block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">The global <inline font-style="italic">rendition</inline> attribute can be attached to any element, and used wherever necessary to specify details of the highlighting used for it in the source. For example, a heading rendered in bold might be tagged <inline>&lt;head rendition="simple:bold"&gt;</inline>, and one in italic <inline>&lt;head rendition="simple:italic"&gt;</inline>.</block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">The values used for the <inline font-style="italic">rendition</inline> attribute point to definitions provided for the formatting concerned. These definitions are typically provided by a <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.rendition" text-decoration="underline">&lt;rendition&gt;</basic-link> element in the document's header, as further discussed in section <basic-link internal-destination="hdr-rend" text-decoration="underline">15.2.3 (Tagging Declaration)</basic-link>.</block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">It is not always possible or desirable to interpret the reasons for such changes of rendering in a text. In such cases, the element <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.hi" text-decoration="underline">&lt;hi&gt;</basic-link> may be used to mark a sequence of highlighted text without making any claim as to its status. <list-block provisional-distance-between-starts="18pt"
                        provisional-label-separation="6pt"
                        text-indent="0em"
                        margin-right="10pt"
                        space-before="6pt"
                        space-after="6pt">
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.hi" text-decoration="underline">hi</basic-link>
                        </inline> (highlighted) marks a word or phrase as graphically distinct from the surrounding text, for reasons concerning which no claim is made.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
            </list-block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">In the following example, the use of a distinct typeface for the subheading and for the included name are recorded but not interpreted: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">
               <inline>&lt;p&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;hi rendition="simple:blackletter"&gt;</inline>And
   this Indenture further witnesseth<inline>&lt;/hi&gt;</inline> that the said <inline>&lt;hi rendition="simple:italic"&gt;</inline>Walter Shandy<inline>&lt;/hi&gt;</inline>, merchant, in consideration of the
 said intended marriage ...
<inline>&lt;/p&gt;</inline>
            </block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">Alternatively, where the cause for the highlighting can be identified with confidence, a number of other, more specific, elements are available. <list-block provisional-distance-between-starts="18pt"
                        provisional-label-separation="6pt"
                        text-indent="0em"
                        margin-right="10pt"
                        space-before="6pt"
                        space-after="6pt">
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.foreign" text-decoration="underline">foreign</basic-link>
                        </inline> identifies a word or phrase as belonging to some language other than that of the surrounding text.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.label" text-decoration="underline">label</basic-link>
                        </inline> contains any label or heading used to identify part of a text, typically but not exclusively in a list or glossary.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.title" text-decoration="underline">title</basic-link>
                        </inline> contains a title for any kind of work.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
            </list-block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">Some features (notably quotations,  titles, and foreign words) may be found in a text either marked by highlighting, or with quotation marks. In either case, the element <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.q" text-decoration="underline">&lt;q&gt;</basic-link>  (as discussed in the following section) should be used. Again, the global <inline font-style="italic">rendition</inline> attribute can be used to record details of the highlighting used in the source if this is thought useful.</block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">As an example of the elements defined here, consider the following sentence: On the one hand the <inline font-style="italic">Nibelungenlied</inline> is associated with the new rise of romance of twelfth-century France, the <inline font-style="italic">romans d'antiquité</inline>, the romances of Chrétien de Troyes, and the German adaptations of these works by Heinrich van Veldeke, Hartmann von Aue, and Wolfram von Eschenbach. Interpreting the role of the highlighting, the sentence might be encoded as follows: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">
               <inline>&lt;p&gt;</inline>On the one hand the
<inline>&lt;title&gt;</inline>Nibelungenlied<inline>&lt;/title&gt;</inline> is associated with the new rise of romance of
 twelfth-century France, the <inline>&lt;foreign&gt;</inline>romans d'antiquité<inline>&lt;/foreign&gt;</inline>, the romances of
 Chrétien de Troyes, ...<inline>&lt;/p&gt;</inline>
            </block> Describing only the appearance of the original, it might be encoded like this: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">
               <inline>&lt;p&gt;</inline>On the one hand the <inline>&lt;hi rendition="simple:italic"&gt;</inline>Nibelungenlied<inline>&lt;/hi&gt;</inline> is associated with the new rise of
 romance of twelfth-century France, the <inline>&lt;hi rendition="simple:italic"&gt;</inline>romans
   d'antiquité<inline>&lt;/hi&gt;</inline>, the romances of Chrétien de Troyes, ...<inline>&lt;/p&gt;</inline>
            </block>
         </block>
         <block keep-with-next.within-page="always"
                id="z635"
                text-align="left"
                font-family="Times"
                font-size="12pt"
                font-weight="bold"
                font-style="italic"
                space-after="2pt"
                space-before.optimum="4pt"
                text-indent="0em">3.5.2. Quotations and Related Features</block>
         <block line-height="1">Like changes of typeface, quotation marks are conventionally used to denote several different features within a text, of which the most frequent is quotation, though many other features are possible. The full TEI Guidelines provide additional elements such as <inline hyphenate="false" color="black" font-family="Courier">&lt;mentioned&gt;</inline> or <inline hyphenate="false" color="black" font-family="Courier">&lt;said&gt;</inline> to distinguish some of these features, but these more specialised elements are not included in TEI simplePrint. In TEI Simple however, we use the <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.quote" text-decoration="underline">&lt;quote&gt;</basic-link> element for quotation only, and the <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.q" text-decoration="underline">&lt;q&gt;</basic-link> element for all other material found within quotation marks in the text.<list-block provisional-distance-between-starts="18pt"
                        provisional-label-separation="6pt"
                        text-indent="0em"
                        margin-right="10pt"
                        space-before="6pt"
                        space-after="6pt">
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.q" text-decoration="underline">q</basic-link>
                        </inline> (quoted) contains material which is distinguished from the surrounding text using quotation marks or a similar method, for any one of a variety of reasons including, but not limited to: direct speech or thought, technical terms or jargon, authorial distance, quotations from elsewhere, and passages that are mentioned but not used.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.quote" text-decoration="underline">quote</basic-link>
                        </inline> (quotation) contains a phrase or passage attributed by the narrator or author to some agency external to the text.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
            </list-block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">Here is a simple example of a quotation: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">
               <inline>&lt;p&gt;</inline>Few dictionary makers are likely to
 forget Dr. Johnson's description of the lexicographer as <inline>&lt;quote&gt;</inline>a harmless
   drudge.<inline>&lt;/quote&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;/p&gt;</inline>
            </block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">As elsewhere, the way that a citation or quotation was printed (for example, <inline font-style="italic" font-weight="normal">in-line</inline> or set off as a <inline font-style="italic">display</inline> or <inline font-style="italic">block quotation</inline>), may be represented using the <inline font-style="italic">rendition</inline> attribute. This may also be used to indicate the kind of quotation marks used.</block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">Direct speech interrupted by a narrator can be represented simply by ending the <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.q" text-decoration="underline">&lt;q&gt;</basic-link> element and beginning it again after the interruption, as in the following example: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">
               <inline>&lt;p&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;q&gt;</inline>Who-e debel
   you?<inline>&lt;/q&gt;</inline> — he at last said — <inline>&lt;q&gt;</inline>you no speak-e, damme, I kill-e.<inline>&lt;/q&gt;</inline> And so
 saying, the lighted tomahawk began flourishing about me in the dark.
<inline>&lt;/p&gt;</inline>
            </block> If it is important to convey the idea that the two <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.q" text-decoration="underline">&lt;q&gt;</basic-link> elements together make up a single speech, the linking attributes <inline font-style="italic">next</inline> and <inline font-style="italic">prev</inline> may be used, as described in section <basic-link internal-destination="xatts" text-decoration="underline">3.7.3 (Special Kinds of Linking)</basic-link>.</block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">Direct speech may be accompanied by a reference to the source or speaker, using the <inline font-style="italic">who</inline> attribute, whether or not this is explicit in the text, as in the following example: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">
               <inline>&lt;q who="#Wilson"&gt;</inline>Spaulding, he came down into the office just this day eight weeks with this very
 paper in his hand, and he says:—<inline>&lt;q who="#Spaulding"&gt;</inline>I wish to the Lord, Mr.
   Wilson, that I was a red-headed man.<inline>&lt;/q&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;/q&gt;</inline>
            </block> This example also demonstrates how quotations may be embedded within other quotations: one speaker (Wilson) quotes another speaker (Spaulding).</block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">The creator of the electronic text must decide whether quotation marks are replaced by the tags or whether the tags are added and the quotation marks kept. If the quotation marks are removed from the text, the <inline font-style="italic">rendition</inline> attribute may be used to record the way in which they were rendered in the copy text.</block>
         <block keep-with-next.within-page="always"
                id="z636"
                text-align="left"
                font-family="Times"
                font-size="12pt"
                font-weight="bold"
                font-style="italic"
                space-after="2pt"
                space-before.optimum="4pt"
                text-indent="0em">3.5.3. Foreign Words or Expressions</block>
         <block line-height="1">Words, phrases, or longer stretches of text that are not in the main language of the texts may be tagged as such in one of two ways. The global <inline font-style="italic">xml:lang</inline> attribute may be attached to any element to show that it uses some other language than that of the surrounding text. Where there is no applicable element, the element <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.foreign" text-decoration="underline">&lt;foreign&gt;</basic-link> may be used, again using the <inline font-style="italic">xml:lang</inline> attribute. For example: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">
               <inline>&lt;p&gt;</inline>John has real <inline>&lt;foreign xml:lang="fr"&gt;</inline>savoir-faire<inline>&lt;/foreign&gt;</inline>.<inline>&lt;/p&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;p&gt;</inline>Have you read <inline>&lt;title xml:lang="de"&gt;</inline>Die Dreigroschenoper<inline>&lt;/title&gt;</inline>?<inline>&lt;/p&gt;</inline>
            </block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">As these examples show, the <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.foreign" text-decoration="underline">&lt;foreign&gt;</basic-link> element should not be used to tag foreign words if some other more specific element such as <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.title" text-decoration="underline">&lt;title&gt;</basic-link>, or <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.div" text-decoration="underline">&lt;div&gt;</basic-link> applies.</block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">The value of the <inline font-style="italic">xml:lang</inline> attribute on an element applies hierarchically to everything contained by that element, unless overridden:</block>
         <block font-family="Courier"
                background-color="lightgray"
                color="black"
                white-space-treatment="preserve"
                linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                white-space-collapse="false"
                wrap-option="no-wrap"
                text-indent="0em"
                hyphenate="false"
                start-indent="12pt"
                text-align="start"
                font-size="6pt"
                space-before.optimum="4pt"
                space-after.optimum="4pt">
            <inline>&lt;div xml:lang="la"&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;p&gt;</inline>Pars haec Latine composita est.<inline>&lt;/p&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;p xml:lang="en"&gt;</inline>Except that this sentence is in English.<inline>&lt;/p&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;p&gt;</inline>Vita brevis, ars longa.<inline>&lt;/p&gt;</inline>
            <inline>&lt;/div&gt;</inline>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">Here we specify that the whole <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.div" text-decoration="underline">&lt;div&gt;</basic-link> element uses the language with the coded identifier <inline>la</inline> i.e., Latin. Since it is contained by that <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.div" text-decoration="underline">&lt;div&gt;</basic-link> there is no need to supply this information again for the first <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.s" text-decoration="underline">&lt;s&gt;</basic-link> element. The second <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.s" text-decoration="underline">&lt;s&gt;</basic-link> element however overrides this value, and indicates that its content is in English (the language with identifier <inline>en</inline>). The third <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.s" text-decoration="underline">&lt;s&gt;</basic-link> element is again in Latin.</block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">The codes used to identify languages, supplied on the <inline font-style="italic">xml:lang</inline> attribute, are defined by an international standard<footnote>
               <inline font-size="7pt" vertical-align="super">3</inline>
               <footnote-body>
                  <block end-indent="0pt"
                         start-indent="0pt"
                         text-align="start"
                         font-style="normal"
                         text-indent="1em"
                         font-size="9pt"
                         line-height="normal">
                     <inline font-size="7pt" vertical-align="super">3</inline> The relevant Internet standard is <inline font-style="italic">Best Current Practice 47</inline> (<basic-link external-destination="url(http://tools.ietf.org/html/bcp47)">http://tools.ietf.org/html/bcp47</basic-link>). The authoritative list of registered subtags is maintained by IANA and is available at <basic-link external-destination="url(http://www.iana.org/assignments/language-subtag-registry)">http://www.iana.org/assignments/language-subtag-registry</basic-link>. For a general overview of the construction of language tags, see <basic-link external-destination="url(http://www.w3.org/International/articles/language-tags/)">http://www.w3.org/International/articles/language-tags/</basic-link>, and for a practical step-by-step guide, see <basic-link external-destination="url(http://www.w3.org/International/questions/qa-choosing-language-tags)">http://www.w3.org/International/questions/qa-choosing-language-tags</basic-link>.</block>
               </footnote-body>
            </footnote>, as further explained in the relevant section of the TEI Guidelines. Some simple example codes for a few languages are given here: <table text-align="left"
                   font-size="9pt"
                   table-layout="fixed"
                   width="100%"
                   id="d27e1540">
               <table-column column-number="1" column-width="10.891089108910892%"/>
               <table-column column-number="2" column-width="39.603960396039604%"/>
               <table-column column-number="3" column-width="10.891089108910892%"/>
               <table-column column-number="4" column-width="38.613861386138616%"/>
               <table-body text-indent="0pt">
                  <table-row>
                     <table-cell padding="2pt" text-align="left">
                        <block>zh</block>
                     </table-cell>
                     <table-cell padding="2pt" text-align="left">
                        <block>Chinese</block>
                     </table-cell>
                     <table-cell padding="2pt" text-align="left">
                        <block>grc</block>
                     </table-cell>
                     <table-cell padding="2pt" text-align="left">
                        <block>Ancient Greek</block>
                     </table-cell>
                  </table-row>
                  <table-row>
                     <table-cell padding="2pt" text-align="left">
                        <block>en</block>
                     </table-cell>
                     <table-cell padding="2pt" text-align="left">
                        <block>English</block>
                     </table-cell>
                     <table-cell padding="2pt" text-align="left">
                        <block>el</block>
                     </table-cell>
                     <table-cell padding="2pt" text-align="left">
                        <block>Greek</block>
                     </table-cell>
                  </table-row>
                  <table-row>
                     <table-cell padding="2pt" text-align="left">
                        <block>enm</block>
                     </table-cell>
                     <table-cell padding="2pt" text-align="left">
                        <block>Middle English</block>
                     </table-cell>
                     <table-cell padding="2pt" text-align="left">
                        <block>ja</block>
                     </table-cell>
                     <table-cell padding="2pt" text-align="left">
                        <block>Japanese</block>
                     </table-cell>
                  </table-row>
                  <table-row>
                     <table-cell padding="2pt" text-align="left">
                        <block>fr</block>
                     </table-cell>
                     <table-cell padding="2pt" text-align="left">
                        <block>French</block>
                     </table-cell>
                     <table-cell padding="2pt" text-align="left">
                        <block>la</block>
                     </table-cell>
                     <table-cell padding="2pt" text-align="left">
                        <block>Latin</block>
                     </table-cell>
                  </table-row>
                  <table-row>
                     <table-cell padding="2pt" text-align="left">
                        <block>de</block>
                     </table-cell>
                     <table-cell padding="2pt" text-align="left">
                        <block>German</block>
                     </table-cell>
                     <table-cell padding="2pt" text-align="left">
                        <block>sa</block>
                     </table-cell>
                     <table-cell padding="2pt" text-align="left">
                        <block>Sanskrit</block>
                     </table-cell>
                  </table-row>
               </table-body>
            </table>
         </block>
         <block keep-with-next.within-page="always"
                id="Simple-notes"
                text-align="left"
                font-family="Times"
                font-size="14pt"
                font-weight="bold"
                space-after="3pt"
                space-before.optimum="9pt"
                text-indent="0em">3.6. Notes</block>
         <block line-height="1">A note is any additional comment found in a text, marked in some way as being out of the main textual stream. A note is always attached to some part of the text, implicitly or explicitly: we call this its target, or its <inline font-style="italic" font-weight="normal">point of attachment</inline>. The element <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.note" text-decoration="underline">&lt;note&gt;</basic-link> should be used to mark any kind of note whether it appears as a separate block of text in the main text area, at the foot of the page, at the end of the chapter or volume, in the margin, or in some other place. <list-block provisional-distance-between-starts="18pt"
                        provisional-label-separation="6pt"
                        text-indent="0em"
                        margin-right="10pt"
                        space-before="6pt"
                        space-after="6pt">
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.note" text-decoration="underline">note</basic-link>
                        </inline> contains a note or annotation.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
            </list-block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">Notes may be in a different hand or typeface, may be authorial or editorial, and may have been added later. The attributes <inline font-style="italic">type</inline> and <inline font-style="italic">resp</inline> can be used to distinguish between different kinds of notes or identify their authors.</block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">In a printed or written text, the point of attachment for a note is typically represented by a siglum such as an alphanumerical or other character, often in superscripted form. When encoding such a text, it is conventional to replace this siglum by a note element containing the annotation itself, as in the following example: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">
               <inline>&lt;p&gt;</inline>[...] some text <inline>&lt;note xml:id="n7"&gt;</inline>a note about some text<inline>&lt;/note&gt;</inline> [...] <inline>&lt;/p&gt;</inline>
            </block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">An alternative approach is to encode the point of attachment wherever it appears in the text, using for example the <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.ref" text-decoration="underline">&lt;ref&gt;</basic-link> element discussed in the next section, and to place the <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.note" text-decoration="underline">&lt;note&gt;</basic-link> element anywhere convenient. The two can then be associated by using the <inline font-style="italic">target</inline> attribute on the <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.ref" text-decoration="underline">&lt;ref&gt;</basic-link> element to point to the <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.note" text-decoration="underline">&lt;note&gt;</basic-link> element, as in the following example, in which the superscripted ‘7’ indicating the point of attachment has been retained as part of the encoding: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">
               <inline>&lt;p&gt;</inline>[...] some text <inline>&lt;ref target="#n7"
  rendition="simple:superscript"&gt;</inline>7<inline>&lt;/ref&gt;</inline> [...] <inline>&lt;note xml:id="n7"&gt;</inline>a note about some text<inline>&lt;/note&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;/p&gt;</inline>
            </block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">It may however be problematic to determine the precise position of the point of attachment, particularly in the case of marginal notes. A marginal note may also be hard to distinguish from a label or subheading which introduces the text with which it is associated. Where the purpose of the note is clearly to label the associated text, rather than to comment on it, the element <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.label" text-decoration="underline">&lt;label&gt;</basic-link> may be preferable. Where it is clearly a subheading attached to a distinct subdivision, it may be preferable to start a new element <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.div" text-decoration="underline">&lt;div&gt;</basic-link> and encode the subheading as a <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.head" text-decoration="underline">&lt;head&gt;</basic-link>. Note however that a <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.head" text-decoration="underline">&lt;head&gt;</basic-link> cannot be inserted anywhere except at the beginning of a <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.div" text-decoration="underline">&lt;div&gt;</basic-link>. And where (as in some Early Modern English plays) marginal annotation is systematically used to identify speakers, it may be better to represent these using the <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.speaker" text-decoration="underline">&lt;speaker&gt;</basic-link> element introduced above. In cases of doubt, the encoder should decide on a clear policy and preferably document it for the use of others.</block>
         <block keep-with-next.within-page="always"
                id="Simple-ptrs"
                text-align="left"
                font-family="Times"
                font-size="14pt"
                font-weight="bold"
                space-after="3pt"
                space-before.optimum="9pt"
                text-indent="0em">3.7. Cross References and Links</block>
         <block line-height="1">Any kind of cross reference or link found at one point in a text which points to another part of the same or another document may be encoded using the <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.ref" text-decoration="underline">&lt;ref&gt;</basic-link> element discussed in this section. Implicit links (such as the association between two parallel texts, or that between a text and its interpretation) may be encoded using the linking attributes discussed in section <basic-link internal-destination="xatts" text-decoration="underline">3.7.3 (Special Kinds of Linking)</basic-link>.</block>
         <block keep-with-next.within-page="always"
                id="ptrs"
                text-align="left"
                font-family="Times"
                font-size="12pt"
                font-weight="bold"
                font-style="italic"
                space-after="2pt"
                space-before.optimum="4pt"
                text-indent="0em">3.7.1. Simple Cross References</block>
         <block line-height="1">
            <list-block provisional-distance-between-starts="18pt"
                        provisional-label-separation="6pt"
                        text-indent="0em"
                        margin-right="10pt"
                        space-before="6pt"
                        space-after="6pt">
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.ref" text-decoration="underline">ref</basic-link>
                        </inline> (reference) defines a reference to another location, possibly modified by additional text or comment.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
            </list-block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">Usually, the presence of a cross-reference or link will be indicated by some text or symbol in the source being encoded, which will then become the content of the <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.ref" text-decoration="underline">&lt;ref&gt;</basic-link> element. Occasionally, however, and frequently in the case of a born digital document, the exact form and appearance of the cross reference text will be determined dynamically by the software processing the document. In such cases, the <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.ref" text-decoration="underline">&lt;ref&gt;</basic-link> element will have no content, and serve simply to mark a point from which a link is to be made, along with the target of the link.</block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">The following two forms, for example, are logically equivalent: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">See especially <inline>&lt;ref target="#SEC12"&gt;</inline>section
 12 on page 34<inline>&lt;/ref&gt;</inline>.</block>
            <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">See especially <inline>&lt;ref target="#SEC12"/&gt;</inline>.</block> In both cases, there is a cross reference from the position in the source document immediately following the word <inline font-style="italic" font-weight="normal">especially</inline> to whatever element in the encoded document has the identifier <inline>SEC12</inline>. In the first case, the encoder has supplied the original form of the cross reference ‘section 12 on page 34’; in the second, the task of generating an appropriate form of cross reference has been left to the formatting software. Perhaps the pagination and section numbers of the document in question are not yet determined; perhaps the cross reference should be replaced by a big red button. In either case, however, the value of the <inline font-style="italic">target</inline> attribute must be the identifier of some other element within the current document. Since the passage or phrase being pointed at must bear an identifier, it must be an element of some kind. In the following example, the cross reference is to a <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.div" text-decoration="underline">&lt;div&gt;</basic-link> element: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt"> ... see especially <inline>&lt;ref target="#SEC12"/&gt;</inline>.
 ... 
<inline>&lt;div xml:id="SEC12"&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;head&gt;</inline>Concerning Identifiers<inline>&lt;/head&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;!-- ... --&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;/div&gt;</inline>
            </block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">Because the <inline font-style="italic">xml:id</inline> attribute is global, any element in a TEI document may be pointed to in this way. In the following example, a paragraph has been given an identifier so that it may be pointed at: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt"> ... this is discussed in <inline>&lt;ref target="#pspec"&gt;</inline>the paragraph on links<inline>&lt;/ref&gt;</inline> ... 
<inline>&lt;p xml:id="pspec"&gt;</inline>Links may be
 made to any kind of element ...<inline>&lt;/p&gt;</inline>
            </block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">Sometimes the target of a cross reference does not correspond with any particular feature of a text, and so may not be tagged as an element of some kind. If the desired target is simply a point in the current document, the easiest way to mark it is by introducing an <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.anchor" text-decoration="underline">&lt;anchor&gt;</basic-link> element at the appropriate spot. If the target is some sequence of words not otherwise tagged, the <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.seg" text-decoration="underline">&lt;seg&gt;</basic-link> element may be used to mark them. These two elements are described as follows: <list-block provisional-distance-between-starts="18pt"
                        provisional-label-separation="6pt"
                        text-indent="0em"
                        margin-right="10pt"
                        space-before="6pt"
                        space-after="6pt">
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.anchor" text-decoration="underline">anchor</basic-link>
                        </inline> (anchor point) attaches an identifier to a point within a text, whether or not it corresponds with a textual element.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.seg" text-decoration="underline">seg</basic-link>
                        </inline> (arbitrary segment) represents any segmentation of text below the ‘chunk’ level.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
            </list-block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">In the following example, <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.ref" text-decoration="underline">&lt;ref&gt;</basic-link> elements have been used to represent points in this text which are to be linked in some way to other parts of it; in the first case to a point, and in the second, to a sequence of words: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt"> Returning to <inline>&lt;ref target="#ABCD"&gt;</inline>the point
 where I dozed off<inline>&lt;/ref&gt;</inline>, I noticed that <inline>&lt;ref target="#EFGH"&gt;</inline>three words<inline>&lt;/ref&gt;</inline> had
 been circled in red by a previous reader</block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">This encoding requires that elements with the specified identifiers (<inline>ABCD</inline> and <inline>EFGH</inline> in this example) are to be found somewhere else in the current document. Assuming that no element already exists to carry these identifiers, the <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.anchor" text-decoration="underline">&lt;anchor&gt;</basic-link> and <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.seg" text-decoration="underline">&lt;seg&gt;</basic-link> elements may be used: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt"> [...] <inline>&lt;anchor type="bookmark" xml:id="ABCD"/&gt;</inline> [...] <inline>&lt;seg type="target" xml:id="EFGH"&gt;</inline> [...] <inline>&lt;/seg&gt;</inline> [...] </block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">The <inline font-style="italic">type</inline> attribute should be used (as above) to distinguish amongst different purposes for which these general purpose elements might be used in a text. Some other uses are discussed in section <basic-link internal-destination="xatts" text-decoration="underline">3.7.3 (Special Kinds of Linking)</basic-link> below.</block>
         <block keep-with-next.within-page="always"
                id="xptrs"
                text-align="left"
                font-family="Times"
                font-size="12pt"
                font-weight="bold"
                font-style="italic"
                space-after="2pt"
                space-before.optimum="4pt"
                text-indent="0em">3.7.2. Pointing to other documents</block>
         <block line-height="1">So far, we have shown how the <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.ref" text-decoration="underline">&lt;ref&gt;</basic-link> element may be used for cross-references or links whose targets occur within the same document as their source. The element may also be used to refer to elements in any other XML document or resource, such as a document on the web, or a database component. This is possible because the value of the <inline font-style="italic">target</inline> attribute may be any valid <inline font-style="italic" font-weight="normal">Uniform Resource Identifier</inline> (URI)<footnote>
               <inline font-size="7pt" vertical-align="super">4</inline>
               <footnote-body>
                  <block end-indent="0pt"
                         start-indent="0pt"
                         text-align="start"
                         font-style="normal"
                         text-indent="1em"
                         font-size="9pt"
                         line-height="normal">
                     <inline font-size="7pt" vertical-align="super">4</inline> A full definition of this term, defined by the W3C (the consortium which manages the development and maintenance of the World Wide Web), is beyond the scope of this tutorial: however, the most frequently encountered version of a URI is the familiar URL used to indicate a web page, such as <inline font-family="Courier">http://www.tei-c.org/index.xml</inline>
                  </block>
               </footnote-body>
            </footnote>.</block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">A URI may reference a web page or just a part of one, for example <inline font-family="Courier">http://www.tei-c.org/index.xml#SEC2</inline>. The hash sign indicates that what follows it is the identifier of an element to be located within the XML document identified by what precedes it: this example will therefore locate an element which has an <inline font-style="italic">xml:id</inline> attribute value of <inline>SEC2</inline> within the document retrieved from <inline font-family="Courier">http://www.tei-c.org/index.xml</inline>. In the examples we have discussed so far, the part to the left of the sharp sign has been omitted: this is understood to mean that the referenced element is to be located within the current document.</block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">It is also possible to define an abbreviated form of the URI, using a predefined <inline font-style="italic" font-weight="normal">prefix</inline> separated from the rest of the code by a colon, as for example <inline>cesr:SEC2</inline>. This is known as a <inline font-style="italic" font-weight="normal">private URI</inline>, since the prefix is not standardized (except that the prefix <inline>xml:</inline> is reserved for use by XML itself). A <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.prefixDef" text-decoration="underline">&lt;prefixDef&gt;</basic-link> element should be supplied within the TEI header specifying how the prefix (here <inline>cesr</inline>) should be translated to give a full URL for the link. This is particularly useful if a document contains many references to an external document such as an authority file.</block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">Parts of an XML document can be specified by means of other more sophisticated mechanisms using a language called Xpointer, also defined by the W3C. This is useful when, for example, the elements to be linked to do not bear identifiers. Further information about this and other forms of link addressing is provided in <basic-link external-destination="url(http://www.tei-c.org/release/doc/tei-p5-doc/en/html/SA.html)">chapter 16 of the TEI Guidelines</basic-link> but is beyond the scope of the present document.</block>
         <block keep-with-next.within-page="always"
                id="xatts"
                text-align="left"
                font-family="Times"
                font-size="12pt"
                font-weight="bold"
                font-style="italic"
                space-after="2pt"
                space-before.optimum="4pt"
                text-indent="0em">3.7.3. Special Kinds of Linking</block>
         <block line-height="1">The following special purpose <inline font-style="italic" font-weight="normal">linking</inline> attributes are defined for every element in the TEI simplePrint schema: <list-block provisional-distance-between-starts="42pt"
                        provisional-label-separation="6pt"
                        text-indent="0em"
                        margin-right="10pt"
                        space-before="6pt"
                        space-after="6pt">
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="start"
                            font-weight="bold"
                            keep-together.within-line="always">
                        <inline font-style="italic">ana</inline>
                     </block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <block> </block>links an element with its interpretation.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="start"
                            font-weight="bold"
                            keep-together.within-line="always">
                        <inline font-style="italic">corresp</inline>
                     </block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <block> </block>links an element with one or more other corresponding elements.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="start"
                            font-weight="bold"
                            keep-together.within-line="always">
                        <inline font-style="italic">next</inline>
                     </block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <block> </block>links an element to the next element in an aggregate.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="start"
                            font-weight="bold"
                            keep-together.within-line="always">
                        <inline font-style="italic">prev</inline>
                     </block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <block> </block>links an element to the previous element in an aggregate.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
            </list-block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">The <inline font-style="italic">ana</inline> (analysis) attribute is intended for use where a set of abstract analyses or interpretations have been defined somewhere within a document, as further discussed in section <basic-link internal-destination="Simple-anal" text-decoration="underline">10 (Analysis)</basic-link>. For example, a linguistic analysis of the sentence ‘John loves Nancy’ might be encoded as follows: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">
               <inline>&lt;seg type="sentence" ana="#SVO"&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;seg type="lex" ana="#NP1"&gt;</inline>John<inline>&lt;/seg&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;seg type="lex" ana="#VVI"&gt;</inline>loves<inline>&lt;/seg&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;seg type="lex" ana="#NP1"&gt;</inline>Nancy<inline>&lt;/seg&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;/seg&gt;</inline>
            </block> This encoding implies the existence elsewhere in the document of elements with identifiers <inline>SVO</inline>, <inline>NP1</inline>, and <inline>VV1</inline> where the significance of these particular codes is explained. Note the use of the <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.seg" text-decoration="underline">&lt;seg&gt;</basic-link> element to mark particular components of the analysis, distinguished by the <inline font-style="italic">type</inline> attribute.</block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">The <inline font-style="italic">corresp</inline> (corresponding) attribute provides a simple way of representing some form of correspondence between two elements in a text. For example, in a multilingual text, it may be used to link translation equivalents, as in the following example: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">
               <inline>&lt;seg xml:lang="fr" xml:id="FR1"
 corresp="#EN1"&gt;</inline>Jean aime Nancy<inline>&lt;/seg&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;seg xml:lang="en" xml:id="EN1"
 corresp="#FR1"&gt;</inline>John loves Nancy<inline>&lt;/seg&gt;</inline>
            </block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">The same mechanism may be used for a variety of purposes. In the following example, it has been used to represent the correspondences between ‘the show’ and ‘Shirley’, and between ‘NBC’ and ‘the network’: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">
               <inline>&lt;p&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;title xml:id="shirley"&gt;</inline>Shirley<inline>&lt;/title&gt;</inline>,
 which made its Friday night debut only a month ago, was not listed on <inline>&lt;name xml:id="nbc"&gt;</inline>NBC<inline>&lt;/name&gt;</inline>'s new schedule,
 although <inline>&lt;seg xml:id="network" corresp="#nbc"&gt;</inline>the network<inline>&lt;/seg&gt;</inline> says <inline>&lt;seg xml:id="show" corresp="#shirley"&gt;</inline>the
   show<inline>&lt;/seg&gt;</inline> still is being considered.
<inline>&lt;/p&gt;</inline>
            </block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">The <inline font-style="italic">next</inline> and <inline font-style="italic">prev</inline> attributes provide a simple way of linking together the components of a discontinuous element, as in the following example: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">
               <inline>&lt;q xml:id="Q1a" next="#Q1b"&gt;</inline>Who-e
 debel you?<inline>&lt;/q&gt;</inline> — he at last said — <inline>&lt;q xml:id="Q1b" prev="#Q1a"&gt;</inline>you no speak-e,
 damme, I kill-e.<inline>&lt;/q&gt;</inline> And so saying, the lighted tomahawk began flourishing about
 me in the dark.</block>
         </block>
         <block keep-with-next.within-page="always"
                id="Simple-edit1"
                text-align="left"
                font-family="Times"
                font-size="18pt"
                font-weight="bold"
                space-after="6pt"
                space-before.optimum="12pt"
                text-indent="0em">4. Editorial Interventions</block>
         <block line-height="1">The process of encoding an electronic text has much in common with the process of editing a manuscript or other text for printed publication. In either case a conscientious editor may wish to record both the original state of the source and any editorial correction or other change made in it. The elements discussed in this and the next section provide some facilities for meeting these needs.</block>
         <block keep-with-next.within-page="always"
                id="d27e1966"
                text-align="left"
                font-family="Times"
                font-size="14pt"
                font-weight="bold"
                space-after="3pt"
                space-before.optimum="9pt"
                text-indent="0em">4.1. Correction and Normalization</block>
         <block line-height="1">The following elements may be used to mark <inline font-style="italic" font-weight="normal">corrections</inline>, that is editorial changes introduced where the editor believes the original to be erroneous: <list-block provisional-distance-between-starts="18pt"
                        provisional-label-separation="6pt"
                        text-indent="0em"
                        margin-right="10pt"
                        space-before="6pt"
                        space-after="6pt">
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.corr" text-decoration="underline">corr</basic-link>
                        </inline> (correction) contains the correct form of a passage apparently erroneous in the copy text.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.sic" text-decoration="underline">sic</basic-link>
                        </inline> (Latin for thus or so) contains text reproduced although apparently incorrect or inaccurate.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
            </list-block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">The following elements may be used to mark <inline font-style="italic" font-weight="normal">normalization</inline>, that is editorial changes introduced for the sake of consistency or modernization of a text: <list-block provisional-distance-between-starts="18pt"
                        provisional-label-separation="6pt"
                        text-indent="0em"
                        margin-right="10pt"
                        space-before="6pt"
                        space-after="6pt">
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.orig" text-decoration="underline">orig</basic-link>
                        </inline> (original form) contains a reading which is marked as following the original, rather than being normalized or corrected.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.reg" text-decoration="underline">reg</basic-link>
                        </inline> (regularization) contains a reading which has been regularized or normalized in some sense.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
            </list-block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">Consider, for example, the following famous passage as it appears in the first quarto printing of Shakespeare's <inline font-style="italic">Henry V</inline>: <block text-align="center" id="d27e2030">
               <external-graphic id="d27e2031"
                                 src="url(./../Images/tableofgreenfields.png)"
                                 content-width="400px"
                                 content-height="100px"
                                 max-width="80%"/>
            </block>
            <block text-align="center"
                   font-style="italic"
                   end-indent="12pt"
                   start-indent="12pt">Figure 1. </block> in particular the phrase we might transcribe directly as <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">... for his Nose was as sharpe as a Pen, and a Table of greene fields</block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">A modern editor might wish to make a number of interventions here, specifically to modernize (or normalize) the Elizabethan spellings of <inline font-style="italic" font-weight="normal">a'</inline> and <inline font-style="italic" font-weight="normal">sharpe</inline> for <inline font-style="italic" font-weight="normal">he</inline> and <inline font-style="italic" font-weight="normal">sharp</inline> respectively. They might also want to emend <inline font-style="italic" font-weight="normal">table</inline> to <inline font-style="italic" font-weight="normal">babbl'd</inline>, following an editorial tradition that goes back to the 18th century Shakespearian scholar Lewis Theobald. The following encoding would then be appropriate: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">... for his Nose was as <inline>&lt;reg&gt;</inline>sharp<inline>&lt;/reg&gt;</inline> as a
 Pen and 
<inline>&lt;reg&gt;</inline>he<inline>&lt;/reg&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;corr resp="#Theobald"&gt;</inline>babbl'd<inline>&lt;/corr&gt;</inline> of green fields</block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">A more conservative or source-oriented editor, however, might want to retain the original, but at the same time signal that some of the readings it contains are in some sense anomalous: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">... for his Nose was as
<inline>&lt;orig&gt;</inline>sharpe<inline>&lt;/orig&gt;</inline> as a Pen, and 
<inline>&lt;orig&gt;</inline>a<inline>&lt;/orig&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;sic&gt;</inline>Table<inline>&lt;/sic&gt;</inline> of green fields</block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">Finally, a modern digital editor may decide to combine both possibilities in a single composite text, using the <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.choice" text-decoration="underline">&lt;choice&gt;</basic-link> element. <list-block provisional-distance-between-starts="18pt"
                        provisional-label-separation="6pt"
                        text-indent="0em"
                        margin-right="10pt"
                        space-before="6pt"
                        space-after="6pt">
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.choice" text-decoration="underline">choice</basic-link>
                        </inline> groups a number of alternative encodings for the same point in a text.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
            </list-block> This allows an editor to indicate where alternative encodings are possible: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">... for his Nose was as <inline>&lt;choice&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;orig&gt;</inline>sharpe<inline>&lt;/orig&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;reg&gt;</inline>sharp<inline>&lt;/reg&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;/choice&gt;</inline> as a Pen, and 
<inline>&lt;choice&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;orig&gt;</inline>a<inline>&lt;/orig&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;reg&gt;</inline>he<inline>&lt;/reg&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;/choice&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;choice&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;corr resp="#Theobald"&gt;</inline>babbl'd<inline>&lt;/corr&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;sic&gt;</inline>Table<inline>&lt;/sic&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;/choice&gt;</inline> of green fields </block>
         </block>
         <block keep-with-next.within-page="always"
                id="Simple-edit2"
                text-align="left"
                font-family="Times"
                font-size="14pt"
                font-weight="bold"
                space-after="3pt"
                space-before.optimum="9pt"
                text-indent="0em">4.2. Omissions, Deletions, and Additions</block>
         <block line-height="1">In addition to correcting or normalizing words and phrases, editors and transcribers may also supply missing material, omit material, or transcribe material deleted or crossed out in the source. In addition, some material may be particularly hard to transcribe because it is hard to make out on the page. The following elements may be used to record such phenomena: <list-block provisional-distance-between-starts="18pt"
                        provisional-label-separation="6pt"
                        text-indent="0em"
                        margin-right="10pt"
                        space-before="6pt"
                        space-after="6pt">
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.add" text-decoration="underline">add</basic-link>
                        </inline> (addition) contains letters, words, or phrases inserted in the source text by an author, scribe, or a previous annotator or corrector.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.gap" text-decoration="underline">gap</basic-link>
                        </inline> indicates a point where material has been omitted in a transcription, whether for editorial reasons described in the TEI header, as part of sampling practice, or because the material is illegible, invisible, or inaudible.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.del" text-decoration="underline">del</basic-link>
                        </inline> (deletion) contains a letter, word, or passage deleted, marked as deleted, or otherwise indicated as superfluous or spurious in the copy text by an author, scribe, or a previous annotator or corrector.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.unclear" text-decoration="underline">unclear</basic-link>
                        </inline> contains a word, phrase, or passage which cannot be transcribed with certainty because it is illegible or inaudible in the source.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.supplied" text-decoration="underline">supplied</basic-link>
                        </inline> signifies text supplied by the transcriber or editor for any reason; for example because the original cannot be read due to physical damage, or because of an obvious omission by the author or scribe.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.subst" text-decoration="underline">subst</basic-link>
                        </inline> (substitution) groups one or more deletions with one or more additions when the combination is to be regarded as a single intervention in the text.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
            </list-block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">These elements may be used to record changes made by an editor, by the transcriber, or (in manuscript material) by the author or scribe. For example, if the source for an electronic text read ‘The following elements are provided for for simple editorial interventions.’ then it might be felt desirable to correct the obvious error, but at the same time to record the deletion of the superfluous second <inline font-style="italic" font-weight="normal">for</inline>, thus: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">The
 following elements are provided for <inline>&lt;del resp="#LB"&gt;</inline>for<inline>&lt;/del&gt;</inline> simple editorial
 interventions.</block> The attribute value <inline>#LB</inline> on the <inline font-style="italic">resp</inline> attribute is used to point to a fuller definition (typically in a <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.respStmt" text-decoration="underline">&lt;respStmt&gt;</basic-link> element) of the person or other agency responsible for correcting the duplication of <inline font-style="italic" font-weight="normal">for</inline>.</block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">If the source read ‘The following elements provided for simple editorial interventions.’ (i.e. if the word <inline font-style="italic" font-weight="normal">are</inline> had been inadvertently dropped) then the scholar identified as <inline>LB</inline> might choose to encode the corrected text as follows: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">The following
 elements <inline>&lt;add resp="#LB"&gt;</inline>are<inline>&lt;/add&gt;</inline> provided for simple editorial
 interventions.</block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">These elements may also be used to record the actual writing process, for example to record passages which have been deleted, added, corrected etc., whether by the author of a literary text or by a scribe copying out a manuscript. An analysis of such documentary modifications may be essential before a reading text can be presented, and is clearly of importance in the editorial process. </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">The example is taken from the surviving authorial manuscript of a poem by the English writer Wilfred Owen, a part of which is shown here: </block>
         <block text-align="center" id="d27e2204">
            <external-graphic id="d27e2205"
                              src="url(./../Images/PHowen.png)"
                              content-width="400px"
                              content-height="100px"
                              max-width="80%"/>
         </block>
         <block text-align="center"
                font-style="italic"
                end-indent="12pt"
                start-indent="12pt">Figure 2. </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">Owen first wrote ‘Helping the worst amongst us’, but then deleted it, adding ‘Dragging the worst amongt us’ over the top. In the same way, he revised the phrase ‘half–blind’ by deleting the ‘half–’ and adding ‘all’ above it. In the last line, he started a word beginning ‘fif’ before deleting it and writing the word ‘five–nines’. We can encode all of this as follows:</block>
         <block font-family="Courier"
                background-color="lightgray"
                color="black"
                white-space-treatment="preserve"
                linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                white-space-collapse="false"
                wrap-option="no-wrap"
                text-indent="0em"
                hyphenate="false"
                start-indent="12pt"
                text-align="start"
                font-size="6pt"
                space-before.optimum="4pt"
                space-after.optimum="4pt">
            <inline>&lt;l&gt;</inline>And towards our distant rest began to trudge,<inline>&lt;/l&gt;</inline>
            <inline>&lt;l&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;subst&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;del&gt;</inline>Helping the worst amongst us<inline>&lt;/del&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;add&gt;</inline>Dragging the worst amongt us<inline>&lt;/add&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;/subst&gt;</inline>, who’d no boots 
<inline>&lt;/l&gt;</inline>
            <inline>&lt;l&gt;</inline>But limped on, blood–shod. All went lame; <inline>&lt;subst&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;del status="shortEnd"&gt;</inline>half–<inline>&lt;/del&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;add&gt;</inline>all<inline>&lt;/add&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;/subst&gt;</inline> blind;<inline>&lt;/l&gt;</inline>
            <inline>&lt;l&gt;</inline>Drunk with fatigue ; deaf even to the hoots<inline>&lt;/l&gt;</inline>
            <inline>&lt;l&gt;</inline>Of tired, outstripped <inline>&lt;del&gt;</inline>fif<inline>&lt;/del&gt;</inline> five–nines that dropped behind.<inline>&lt;/l&gt;</inline>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">The tags <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.add" text-decoration="underline">&lt;add&gt;</basic-link> and <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.del" text-decoration="underline">&lt;del&gt;</basic-link> elements are used to enclose passages added or deleted respectively. Additional attributes are available such as <inline font-style="italic">resp</inline> to indicate responsibility for the modification, or <inline font-style="italic">place</inline> to indicate where in the text (for example, above or below the line) the modification has been made. Where the encoder wishes to assert that the addition and deletion make up a single editorial act of substitution, these elements can be combined within a <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.subst" text-decoration="underline">&lt;subst&gt;</basic-link> element as shown above.</block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">A very careful examination of Owen’s second modification shows that he really did write ‘amongt’ rather than ‘amongst’, presumably in error. An equally careful editor wishing to restore the missing ‘s’ might use the <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.supplied" text-decoration="underline">&lt;supplied&gt;</basic-link> element to indicate that they have done so:</block>
         <block font-family="Courier"
                background-color="lightgray"
                color="black"
                white-space-treatment="preserve"
                linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                white-space-collapse="false"
                wrap-option="no-wrap"
                text-indent="0em"
                hyphenate="false"
                start-indent="12pt"
                text-align="start"
                font-size="6pt"
                space-before.optimum="4pt"
                space-after.optimum="4pt">
            <inline>&lt;add&gt;</inline>Dragging the worst among<inline>&lt;supplied resp="#ED"&gt;</inline>s<inline>&lt;/supplied&gt;</inline>t us<inline>&lt;/add&gt;</inline>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">Here the <inline font-style="italic">resp</inline> attribute has been used to indicate that the ‘s’ was not supplied by Owen but by someone else, specifically the person documented elsewhere by an element with the identifier <inline>ED</inline>.</block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">The <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.unclear" text-decoration="underline">&lt;unclear&gt;</basic-link> element is useful where material in the source is so hard to read that the transcriber is uncertain as to whether they have done so correctly. The <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.gap" text-decoration="underline">&lt;gap&gt;</basic-link> element by contrast should be used where the material is so illegible that the transcriber does not wish even to attempt it. The two may however be used together as in the following example: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">One hundred &amp;amp; twenty good regulars joined <inline>&lt;unclear&gt;</inline>to me <inline>&lt;gap extent="2 words"
  reason="indecipherable"/&gt;</inline> and <inline>&lt;/unclear&gt;</inline> instantly, would aid me signally in an
 enterprise against Wilmington.</block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">The <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.del" text-decoration="underline">&lt;del&gt;</basic-link> element marks material which is deleted in a source, but has been transcribed as part of the electronic text all the same, while <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.gap" text-decoration="underline">&lt;gap&gt;</basic-link> marks the location of source material which is omitted from the electronic text, whether it is legible or not. A language corpus, for example, might omit long quotations in foreign languages. An <inline font-style="italic">extent</inline> attribute is available on the <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.gap" text-decoration="underline">&lt;gap&gt;</basic-link> element to indicate how much material has been omitted. The <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.desc" text-decoration="underline">&lt;desc&gt;</basic-link> element can be used inside the <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.gap" text-decoration="underline">&lt;gap&gt;</basic-link> element to provide a brief characterisation of the omitted material, as in the following examples: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">
               <inline>&lt;p&gt;</inline> ... An example of a list appearing in a fief ledger of <inline>&lt;name type="place"&gt;</inline>Koldinghus<inline>&lt;/name&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;date&gt;</inline>1611/12<inline>&lt;/date&gt;</inline> is given below. It shows cash income from a sale of
 honey.<inline>&lt;/p&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;gap extent="50 lines"&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;desc&gt;</inline>quotation from ledger (in Danish)<inline>&lt;/desc&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;/gap&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;p&gt;</inline>A description of the overall structure of the account is once again ...
<inline>&lt;/p&gt;</inline>
            </block> (The <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.name" text-decoration="underline">&lt;name&gt;</basic-link> and <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.date" text-decoration="underline">&lt;date&gt;</basic-link> elements used in this example are discussed further below)</block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">Language corpora (particular those constructed before the widespread use of scanners) often systematically omit figures and mathematics: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">
               <inline>&lt;p&gt;</inline>At the bottom of your screen below the
 mode line is the <inline>&lt;hi&gt;</inline>minibuffer<inline>&lt;/hi&gt;</inline>. This is the area where Emacs echoes the
 commands you enter and where you specify filenames for Emacs to find, values for
 search and replace, and so on. <inline>&lt;gap reason="graphic"&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;desc&gt;</inline>diagram of Emacs screen<inline>&lt;/desc&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;/gap&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;/p&gt;</inline>
            </block>
         </block>
         <block keep-with-next.within-page="always"
                id="d27e2353"
                text-align="left"
                font-family="Times"
                font-size="14pt"
                font-weight="bold"
                space-after="3pt"
                space-before.optimum="9pt"
                text-indent="0em">4.3. Abbreviation and their Expansion</block>
         <block line-height="1">Like names, dates, and numbers, abbreviations may be transcribed as they stand or expanded; they may be left unmarked, or encoded using the following elements: <list-block provisional-distance-between-starts="18pt"
                        provisional-label-separation="6pt"
                        text-indent="0em"
                        margin-right="10pt"
                        space-before="6pt"
                        space-after="6pt">
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.abbr" text-decoration="underline">abbr</basic-link>
                        </inline> (abbreviation) contains an abbreviation of any sort.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.expan" text-decoration="underline">expan</basic-link>
                        </inline> (expansion) contains the expansion of an abbreviation.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
            </list-block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">The <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.abbr" text-decoration="underline">&lt;abbr&gt;</basic-link> element is useful as a means of distinguishing semi-lexical items such as acronyms or jargon: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">Every
 manufacturer of <inline>&lt;abbr&gt;</inline>3GL<inline>&lt;/abbr&gt;</inline> or <inline>&lt;abbr&gt;</inline>4GL<inline>&lt;/abbr&gt;</inline> languages is currently nailing on
<inline>&lt;abbr&gt;</inline>OOP<inline>&lt;/abbr&gt;</inline> extensions</block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">The <inline font-style="italic">type</inline> attribute may be used to distinguish types of abbreviation by their function.</block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">The <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.expan" text-decoration="underline">&lt;expan&gt;</basic-link> element is used to mark an expansion supplied by an encoder. This element is particularly useful in the transcription of manuscript materials. For example, the character p with a bar through its descender as a conventional representation for the word <inline>per</inline> is commonly encountered in Medieval European manuscripts. An encoder may choose to expand this as follows: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">
               <inline>&lt;expan&gt;</inline>per<inline>&lt;/expan&gt;</inline>
            </block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">To record both an abbreviation and its expansion, the <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.choice" text-decoration="underline">&lt;choice&gt;</basic-link> element mentioned above may be used to group the abbreviated form with its proposed expansion: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">
               <inline>&lt;choice&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;abbr&gt;</inline>wt<inline>&lt;/abbr&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;expan&gt;</inline>with<inline>&lt;/expan&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;/choice&gt;</inline>
            </block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">The elements <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.expan" text-decoration="underline">&lt;expan&gt;</basic-link> and <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.abbr" text-decoration="underline">&lt;abbr&gt;</basic-link> should contain a full word, or the abbreviated form of a full word respectively.  For a fuller discussion of abbreviations and the intricacies of representing them consult the section on <basic-link external-destination="url(http://www.tei-c.org/release/doc/tei-p5-doc/en/html/PH.html#PHAB)">Abbreviation and Expansion</basic-link> in the TEI Guidelines.</block>
         <block keep-with-next.within-page="always"
                id="Simple-names"
                text-align="left"
                font-family="Times"
                font-size="18pt"
                font-weight="bold"
                space-after="6pt"
                space-before.optimum="12pt"
                text-indent="0em">5. Names, Codes, and Numbers</block>
         <block line-height="1">The TEI scheme defines elements for a large number of ‘data-like’ features which may appear almost anywhere within almost any kind of text. These features may be of particular interest in a range of disciplines; they all relate to objects external to the text itself, such as the names of persons and places, strings of code, formulae, or numbers and dates. These items may also pose particular problems for natural language processing (NLP) applications. The elements described here, by making such features explicit, reduce the complexity of processing texts containing them.</block>
         <block keep-with-next.within-page="always"
                id="nomen"
                text-align="left"
                font-family="Times"
                font-size="14pt"
                font-weight="bold"
                space-after="3pt"
                space-before.optimum="9pt"
                text-indent="0em">5.1. Names and Referring Strings</block>
         <block line-height="1">A <inline font-style="italic" font-weight="normal">referring string</inline> is any phrase which refers to some person, place, object, etc. A <inline font-style="italic" font-weight="normal">name</inline> is a referring string which contains proper nouns and honorifics only. Two elements are provided to mark such strings: <list-block provisional-distance-between-starts="18pt"
                        provisional-label-separation="6pt"
                        text-indent="0em"
                        margin-right="10pt"
                        space-before="6pt"
                        space-after="6pt">
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.rs" text-decoration="underline">rs</basic-link>
                        </inline> (referencing string) contains a general purpose name or referring string.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.name" text-decoration="underline">name</basic-link>
                        </inline> (name, proper noun) contains a proper noun or noun phrase.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
            </list-block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">The <inline font-style="italic">type</inline> attribute is used to distinguish amongst (for example) names of persons, places and organizations, where this is possible: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">
               <inline>&lt;q&gt;</inline>My dear <inline>&lt;name type="person"&gt;</inline>Mr.
   Bennet<inline>&lt;/name&gt;</inline>, <inline>&lt;/q&gt;</inline>said his lady to him one day, 
<inline>&lt;q&gt;</inline>have you heard that <inline>&lt;name type="place"&gt;</inline>Netherfield Park<inline>&lt;/name&gt;</inline> is let at last?<inline>&lt;/q&gt;</inline>
            </block>
            <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">It being one of the principles of the
<inline>&lt;name type="organization"&gt;</inline>Circumlocution Office<inline>&lt;/name&gt;</inline> never, on any account
 whatsoever, to give a straightforward answer, <inline>&lt;name type="person"&gt;</inline>Mr Barnacle<inline>&lt;/name&gt;</inline>
 said, 
<inline>&lt;q&gt;</inline>Possibly.<inline>&lt;/q&gt;</inline>
            </block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">As the following example shows, the <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.rs" text-decoration="underline">&lt;rs&gt;</basic-link> element may be used for a reference to a person, place, etc., which does not contain a proper noun or noun phrase: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">
               <inline>&lt;q&gt;</inline>My dear <inline>&lt;name type="person"&gt;</inline>Mr.
   Bennet<inline>&lt;/name&gt;</inline>,<inline>&lt;/q&gt;</inline> said <inline>&lt;rs type="person"&gt;</inline>his lady<inline>&lt;/rs&gt;</inline> to him one day...</block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">Simply tagging something as a name is rarely enough to enable automatic processing of personal names into the canonical forms usually required for reference purposes. The name as it appears in the text may be inconsistently spelled, partial, or vague. Moreover, name prefixes such as <inline font-style="italic" font-weight="normal">van</inline> or <inline font-style="italic" font-weight="normal">de la</inline>, may or may not be included as part of the reference form of a name, depending on the language and country of origin of the bearer.</block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">The <inline font-style="italic">ref</inline> attribute provides a way of linking a name with a description of the object being named, and may thus act as a normalized identifier for it. It is also very useful as a means of gathering together all references to the same individual or location scattered throughout a document: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">
               <inline>&lt;q&gt;</inline>My dear <inline>&lt;name type="person" ref="#BENM1"&gt;</inline>Mr. Bennet<inline>&lt;/name&gt;</inline>, <inline>&lt;/q&gt;</inline> said <inline>&lt;rs type="person" ref="#BENM2"&gt;</inline>his lady<inline>&lt;/rs&gt;</inline> to him
 one day, 
<inline>&lt;q&gt;</inline>have you heard that <inline>&lt;name type="place" ref="#NETP1"&gt;</inline>Netherfield
   Park<inline>&lt;/name&gt;</inline> is let at last?<inline>&lt;/q&gt;</inline>
            </block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">The values used for the <inline font-style="italic">ref</inline> attribute here (<inline>#BENM1</inline> etc.) are pointers; in this case indicating an element with the identifier <inline>BENM1</inline> etc. somewhere in the current document, though any form of URI could be used. The element indicated will typically (for a person) be a <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.person" text-decoration="underline">&lt;person&gt;</basic-link> element, listed within a <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.particDesc" text-decoration="underline">&lt;particDesc&gt;</basic-link> element, or (for a place) a <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.place" text-decoration="underline">&lt;place&gt;</basic-link> element, listed within a <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.settingDesc" text-decoration="underline">&lt;settingDesc&gt;</basic-link> element in the TEI header, as further discussed in <basic-link internal-destination="profDesc" text-decoration="underline">15.3 (The Profile Description)</basic-link> below.</block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">This use should be distinguished from the case of the <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.reg" text-decoration="underline">&lt;reg&gt;</basic-link> (regularization) element, which provides a means of marking the standard form of a referencing string as demonstrated below: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">
               <inline>&lt;name type="person" ref="#WADLM1"&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;choice&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;orig&gt;</inline>Walter de la Mare<inline>&lt;/orig&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;reg&gt;</inline>de la Mare, Walter<inline>&lt;/reg&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;/choice&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;/name&gt;</inline> was born at <inline>&lt;name key="Ch1" type="place"&gt;</inline>Charlton<inline>&lt;/name&gt;</inline>, in <inline>&lt;name key="KT1" type="county"&gt;</inline>Kent<inline>&lt;/name&gt;</inline>, in 1873.</block>
         </block>
         <block keep-with-next.within-page="always"
                id="code"
                text-align="left"
                font-family="Times"
                font-size="14pt"
                font-weight="bold"
                space-after="3pt"
                space-before.optimum="9pt"
                text-indent="0em">5.2. Formulae, Codes, and Special Characters</block>
         <block line-height="1">The following elements may be useful when marking up sequences of text that represent mathematical expressions, chemical formulae, and the like: <list-block provisional-distance-between-starts="18pt"
                        provisional-label-separation="6pt"
                        text-indent="0em"
                        margin-right="10pt"
                        space-before="6pt"
                        space-after="6pt">
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.formula" text-decoration="underline">formula</basic-link>
                        </inline> contains a mathematical or other formula.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.g" text-decoration="underline">g</basic-link>
                        </inline> (character or glyph) represents a glyph, or a non-standard character.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
            </list-block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">In many cases, a simple Unicode character suffices to represent the superscript or subscript digits and other symbols which may appear inside a mathematical formula: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">
               <inline>&lt;formula&gt;</inline>E=mc²<inline>&lt;/formula&gt;</inline>
            </block> In other more complex cases, the encoder may choose to use a different XML scheme (such as MathML) to encode the content of a formula, or a non-XML notation. These possibilities are not discussed further here.</block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">The <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.g" text-decoration="underline">&lt;g&gt;</basic-link> element is useful in the case that no Unicode character exists to represent the character or glyph required. Its <inline font-style="italic">ref</inline> attribute can be used to point to a definition of the symbol intended, while its content (if any) represents a Unicode approximation to it: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">...Thereto
<inline>&lt;g ref="#air"&gt;</inline>[air]<inline>&lt;/g&gt;</inline> and ... </block> The TEI header provides a number of additional elements for the definition of such non-Unicode characters, as further discussed in section <basic-link internal-destination="chardecl" text-decoration="underline">15.2.5 (The character declaration)</basic-link> below.</block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">The following elements are useful for stretches of code or similar formal language appearing within a text: <list-block provisional-distance-between-starts="18pt"
                        provisional-label-separation="6pt"
                        text-indent="0em"
                        margin-right="10pt"
                        space-before="6pt"
                        space-after="6pt">
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.code" text-decoration="underline">code</basic-link>
                        </inline> contains literal code from some formal language such as a programming language.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.email" text-decoration="underline">email</basic-link>
                        </inline> (electronic mail address) contains an email address identifying a location to which email messages can be delivered.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
            </list-block>
         </block>
         <block font-family="Courier"
                background-color="lightgray"
                color="black"
                white-space-treatment="preserve"
                linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                white-space-collapse="false"
                wrap-option="no-wrap"
                text-indent="0em"
                hyphenate="false"
                start-indent="12pt"
                text-align="start"
                font-size="6pt"
                space-before.optimum="4pt"
                space-after.optimum="4pt"> This can be expressed in XML as follows:
<inline>&lt;code&gt;</inline>&amp;amp;lt;date notBefore="2016-06-23"/&gt;<inline>&lt;/code&gt;</inline> Contact the author at

<inline>&lt;email&gt;</inline>lou.burnard@gmail.com<inline>&lt;/email&gt;</inline>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">Note in this example that characters which have a syntactic function in XML (such as the ampersand or the angle bracket) must be represented within a TEI simplePrint document by means of an entity reference such as <inline font-family="Courier">&amp;lt;</inline> or <inline font-family="Courier">&amp;amp;</inline>.</block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">The element <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.ref" text-decoration="underline">&lt;ref&gt;</basic-link> discussed in section <basic-link internal-destination="Simple-ptrs" text-decoration="underline">3.7 (Cross References and Links)</basic-link> should be used to represent a coded reference such as a link given as a URL within a text, either as content or as an attribute value: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">
               <inline>&lt;p&gt;</inline>Further discussion of <inline>&lt;ref target="http://www.tei-c.org/"&gt;</inline>the Text Encoding
   Initiative website<inline>&lt;/ref&gt;</inline> may be found at <inline>&lt;ref&gt;</inline>http://www.tei-c.org/<inline>&lt;/ref&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;/p&gt;</inline>
            </block>
         </block>
         <block keep-with-next.within-page="always"
                id="d27e2676"
                text-align="left"
                font-family="Times"
                font-size="14pt"
                font-weight="bold"
                space-after="3pt"
                space-before.optimum="9pt"
                text-indent="0em">5.3. Dates and Times</block>
         <block line-height="1">The following elements are provided for the detailed encoding of times and dates: <list-block provisional-distance-between-starts="18pt"
                        provisional-label-separation="6pt"
                        text-indent="0em"
                        margin-right="10pt"
                        space-before="6pt"
                        space-after="6pt">
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.date" text-decoration="underline">date</basic-link>
                        </inline> contains a date in any format.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.time" text-decoration="underline">time</basic-link>
                        </inline> contains a phrase defining a time of day in any format.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
            </list-block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">These elements have a number of attributes which can be used to provide normalized versions of their values in various ways. <list-block provisional-distance-between-starts="18pt"
                        provisional-label-separation="6pt"
                        text-indent="0em"
                        margin-right="10pt"
                        space-before="6pt"
                        space-after="6pt">
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.att.datable" text-decoration="underline">att.datable</basic-link>
                        </inline> provides attributes for normalization of elements that contain dates, times, or datable events.<table text-align="left"
                               font-size="9pt"
                               table-layout="fixed"
                               width="100%"
                               id="d27e2708">
                           <table-column column-number="1" column-width="17.880794701986755%"/>
                           <table-column column-number="2" column-width="82.11920529801324%"/>
                           <table-body text-indent="0pt">
                              <table-row>
                                 <table-cell padding="2pt" text-align="left">
                                    <block>
                                       <inline font-style="italic">period</inline>
                                    </block>
                                 </table-cell>
                                 <table-cell padding="2pt" text-align="left">
                                    <block>supplies a pointer to some location defining a named period of time within which the datable item is understood to have occurred.</block>
                                 </table-cell>
                              </table-row>
                              <table-row>
                                 <table-cell padding="2pt" text-align="left">
                                    <block>
                                       <inline font-style="italic">when [att.datable.w3c]</inline>
                                    </block>
                                 </table-cell>
                                 <table-cell padding="2pt" text-align="left">
                                    <block>supplies the value of the date or time in a standard form, e.g. yyyy-mm-dd.</block>
                                 </table-cell>
                              </table-row>
                              <table-row>
                                 <table-cell padding="2pt" text-align="left">
                                    <block>
                                       <inline font-style="italic">notBefore [att.datable.w3c]</inline>
                                    </block>
                                 </table-cell>
                                 <table-cell padding="2pt" text-align="left">
                                    <block>specifies the earliest possible date for the event in standard form, e.g. yyyy-mm-dd.</block>
                                 </table-cell>
                              </table-row>
                              <table-row>
                                 <table-cell padding="2pt" text-align="left">
                                    <block>
                                       <inline font-style="italic">notAfter [att.datable.w3c]</inline>
                                    </block>
                                 </table-cell>
                                 <table-cell padding="2pt" text-align="left">
                                    <block>specifies the latest possible date for the event in standard form, e.g. yyyy-mm-dd.</block>
                                 </table-cell>
                              </table-row>
                           </table-body>
                        </table>
                     </block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
            </list-block> The <inline font-style="italic">when</inline> attribute specifies a normalized form for the date or time, using one of the standard formats defined by ISO 8601. Partial dates or times (e.g. ‘1990’, ‘September 1990’, ‘twelvish’) can be expressed by omitting a part of the value supplied, as in the following examples: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">
               <inline>&lt;date when="1980-02-21"&gt;</inline>21 Feb
 1980<inline>&lt;/date&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;date when="1990"&gt;</inline>1990<inline>&lt;/date&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;date when="1990-09"&gt;</inline>September
 1990<inline>&lt;/date&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;date when="--09"&gt;</inline>September<inline>&lt;/date&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;date when="2001-09-11T12:48:00"&gt;</inline>Sept
 11th, 12 minutes before 9 am<inline>&lt;/date&gt;</inline>
            </block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">These attributes are typically used to make a date or time more easily processable, as in the following examples: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">Given on the
<inline>&lt;date when="1977-06-12"&gt;</inline>Twelfth Day of June in the Year of Our Lord One Thousand
 Nine Hundred and Seventy-seven of the Republic the Two Hundredth and first and of
 the University the Eighty-Sixth.<inline>&lt;/date&gt;</inline>
            </block>
            <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">
               <inline>&lt;l&gt;</inline>specially when it's nine below zero<inline>&lt;/l&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;l&gt;</inline>and <inline>&lt;time when="15:00:00"&gt;</inline>three o'clock in the afternoon<inline>&lt;/time&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;/l&gt;</inline>
            </block> They are also useful in cases where the date concerned is uncertain or only vaguely specified: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">
               <inline>&lt;p&gt;</inline>... <inline>&lt;date period="secondEmpire"&gt;</inline>during the second empire<inline>&lt;/date&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;/p&gt;</inline>
            </block>
            <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">
               <inline>&lt;date notAfter="1946-12-09"
 notBefore="1946-11-01"&gt;</inline>in the weeks shortly before my
 birth<inline>&lt;/date&gt;</inline>
            </block>
         </block>
         <block keep-with-next.within-page="always"
                id="d27e2784"
                text-align="left"
                font-family="Times"
                font-size="14pt"
                font-weight="bold"
                space-after="3pt"
                space-before.optimum="9pt"
                text-indent="0em">5.4. Numbers and Measurements</block>
         <block line-height="1">Like dates, both numbers and quantities can be written with either letters or digits and may therefore need to be normalized for ease of processing. Their presentation is also highly language-dependent (e.g. English <inline font-style="italic" font-weight="normal">5th</inline> becomes Greek <inline font-style="italic" font-weight="normal">5.</inline>; English <inline font-style="italic" font-weight="normal">123,456.78</inline> equals French <inline font-style="italic" font-weight="normal">123.456,78</inline>).</block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">The following elements are provided for the detailed encoding of numbers and quantities: <list-block provisional-distance-between-starts="18pt"
                        provisional-label-separation="6pt"
                        text-indent="0em"
                        margin-right="10pt"
                        space-before="6pt"
                        space-after="6pt">
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.num" text-decoration="underline">num</basic-link>
                        </inline> (number) contains a number, written in any form.<table text-align="left"
                               font-size="9pt"
                               table-layout="fixed"
                               width="100%"
                               id="d27e2811">
                           <table-body text-indent="0pt">
                              <table-row>
                                 <table-cell padding="2pt" text-align="left">
                                    <block>
                                       <inline font-style="italic">type</inline>
                                    </block>
                                 </table-cell>
                                 <table-cell padding="2pt" text-align="left">
                                    <block>indicates the type of numeric value.</block>
                                 </table-cell>
                              </table-row>
                              <table-row>
                                 <table-cell padding="2pt" text-align="left">
                                    <block>
                                       <inline font-style="italic">value</inline>
                                    </block>
                                 </table-cell>
                                 <table-cell padding="2pt" text-align="left">
                                    <block>supplies the value of the number in standard form.</block>
                                 </table-cell>
                              </table-row>
                           </table-body>
                        </table>
                     </block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.measure" text-decoration="underline">measure</basic-link>
                        </inline> contains a word or phrase referring to some quantity of an object or commodity, usually comprising a number, a unit, and a commodity name.<table text-align="left"
                               font-size="9pt"
                               table-layout="fixed"
                               width="100%"
                               id="d27e2834">
                           <table-column column-number="1" column-width="25.33783783783784%"/>
                           <table-column column-number="2" column-width="74.66216216216216%"/>
                           <table-body text-indent="0pt">
                              <table-row>
                                 <table-cell padding="2pt" text-align="left">
                                    <block>
                                       <inline font-style="italic">quantity [att.measurement]</inline>
                                    </block>
                                 </table-cell>
                                 <table-cell padding="2pt" text-align="left">
                                    <block>specifies the number of the specified units that comprise the measurement</block>
                                 </table-cell>
                              </table-row>
                              <table-row>
                                 <table-cell padding="2pt" text-align="left">
                                    <block>
                                       <inline font-style="italic">unit [att.measurement]</inline>
                                    </block>
                                 </table-cell>
                                 <table-cell padding="2pt" text-align="left">
                                    <block>indicates the units used for the measurement, usually using the standard symbol for the desired units.</block>
                                 </table-cell>
                              </table-row>
                              <table-row>
                                 <table-cell padding="2pt" text-align="left">
                                    <block>
                                       <inline font-style="italic">commodity [att.measurement]</inline>
                                    </block>
                                 </table-cell>
                                 <table-cell padding="2pt" text-align="left">
                                    <block>indicates the substance that is being measured</block>
                                 </table-cell>
                              </table-row>
                           </table-body>
                        </table>
                     </block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
            </list-block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">For example: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">
               <inline>&lt;num value="33"&gt;</inline>xxxiii<inline>&lt;/num&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;num type="cardinal" value="21"&gt;</inline>twenty-one<inline>&lt;/num&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;num type="percentage" value="10"&gt;</inline>ten percent<inline>&lt;/num&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;num type="percentage" value="10"&gt;</inline>10%<inline>&lt;/num&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;num type="ordinal" value="5"&gt;</inline>5th<inline>&lt;/num&gt;</inline>
            </block>
            <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">
               <inline>&lt;measure quantity="40" unit="hogshead"
 commodity="rum"&gt;</inline>2 score hh rum<inline>&lt;/measure&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;measure quantity="1" unit="dozen"
 commodity="blooms"&gt;</inline>1 doz. roses<inline>&lt;/measure&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;measure quantity="1" unit="count"
 commodity="blooms"&gt;</inline>a yellow tulip<inline>&lt;/measure&gt;</inline>
            </block>
         </block>
         <block keep-with-next.within-page="always"
                id="Simple-lists"
                text-align="left"
                font-family="Times"
                font-size="18pt"
                font-weight="bold"
                space-after="6pt"
                space-before.optimum="12pt"
                text-indent="0em">6. Lists</block>
         <block line-height="1">The element <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.list" text-decoration="underline">&lt;list&gt;</basic-link> is used to mark any kind of <inline font-style="italic" font-weight="normal">list</inline>. A list is a sequence of text items, which may be numbered, bulleted, or arranged as a glossary list. Each item may be preceded by an item label (in a glossary list, this label is the term being defined): <list-block provisional-distance-between-starts="18pt"
                        provisional-label-separation="6pt"
                        text-indent="0em"
                        margin-right="10pt"
                        space-before="6pt"
                        space-after="6pt">
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.list" text-decoration="underline">list</basic-link>
                        </inline> contains any sequence of items organized as a list.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.item" text-decoration="underline">item</basic-link>
                        </inline> contains one component of a list.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.label" text-decoration="underline">label</basic-link>
                        </inline> contains any label or heading used to identify part of a text, typically but not exclusively in a list or glossary.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
            </list-block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">Individual list items are tagged with <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.item" text-decoration="underline">&lt;item&gt;</basic-link>. The first <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.item" text-decoration="underline">&lt;item&gt;</basic-link> may optionally be preceded by a <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.head" text-decoration="underline">&lt;head&gt;</basic-link>, which gives a heading for the list. The numbering of items within the list may be omitted, indicated using the <inline font-style="italic">n</inline> attribute on each item, or (rarely) tagged as content using the <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.label" text-decoration="underline">&lt;label&gt;</basic-link> element. The following are all thus equivalent: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">
               <inline>&lt;list&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;head&gt;</inline>A short list<inline>&lt;/head&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;item&gt;</inline>First item in list.<inline>&lt;/item&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;item&gt;</inline>Second item in list.<inline>&lt;/item&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;item&gt;</inline>Third item in list.<inline>&lt;/item&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;/list&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;list&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;head&gt;</inline>A short list<inline>&lt;/head&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;item n="1"&gt;</inline>First item in list.<inline>&lt;/item&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;item n="2"&gt;</inline>Second item in list.<inline>&lt;/item&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;item n="3"&gt;</inline>Third item in list.<inline>&lt;/item&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;/list&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;list&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;head&gt;</inline>A short list<inline>&lt;/head&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;label&gt;</inline>1<inline>&lt;/label&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;item&gt;</inline>First item in list.<inline>&lt;/item&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;label&gt;</inline>2<inline>&lt;/label&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;item&gt;</inline>Second item in list.<inline>&lt;/item&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;label&gt;</inline>3<inline>&lt;/label&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;item&gt;</inline>Third item in list.<inline>&lt;/item&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;/list&gt;</inline>
            </block> The styles should not be mixed in the same list.</block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">A simple two-column table may be treated as a <inline font-style="italic" font-weight="normal">glossary list</inline>, tagged <inline>&lt;list type="gloss"&gt;</inline>. Here, each item comprises a <inline font-style="italic" font-weight="normal">term</inline> and a <inline font-style="italic" font-weight="normal">gloss</inline>, marked with <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.label" text-decoration="underline">&lt;label&gt;</basic-link> and <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.item" text-decoration="underline">&lt;item&gt;</basic-link> respectively. <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">
               <inline>&lt;list type="gloss"&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;head&gt;</inline>Vocabulary<inline>&lt;/head&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;label xml:lang="enm"&gt;</inline>nu<inline>&lt;/label&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;item&gt;</inline>now<inline>&lt;/item&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;label xml:lang="enm"&gt;</inline>lhude<inline>&lt;/label&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;item&gt;</inline>loudly<inline>&lt;/item&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;label xml:lang="enm"&gt;</inline>bloweth<inline>&lt;/label&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;item&gt;</inline>blooms<inline>&lt;/item&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;label xml:lang="enm"&gt;</inline>med<inline>&lt;/label&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;item&gt;</inline>meadow<inline>&lt;/item&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;label xml:lang="enm"&gt;</inline>wude<inline>&lt;/label&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;item&gt;</inline>wood<inline>&lt;/item&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;label xml:lang="enm"&gt;</inline>awe<inline>&lt;/label&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;item&gt;</inline>ewe<inline>&lt;/item&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;!-- &lt;label xml:lang="enm"&gt;lhouth&lt;/label&gt;
              &lt;item&gt;lows&lt;/item&gt;
              &lt;label xml:lang="enm"&gt;sterteth&lt;/label&gt;
              &lt;item&gt;bounds, frisks&lt;/item&gt;
              &lt;label xml:lang="enm"&gt;verteth&lt;/label&gt;
              &lt;item xml:lang="la"&gt;pedit&lt;/item&gt;
              &lt;label xml:lang="enm"&gt;murie&lt;/label&gt;
              &lt;item&gt;merrily&lt;/item&gt;
              &lt;label xml:lang="enm"&gt;swik&lt;/label&gt;
              &lt;item&gt;cease&lt;/item&gt;
              &lt;label xml:lang="enm"&gt;naver&lt;/label&gt;
              &lt;item&gt;never&lt;/item&gt;--&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;/list&gt;</inline>
            </block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">Where the internal structure of a list item is more complex, it may be preferable to regard the list as a <inline font-style="italic" font-weight="normal">table</inline>, for which special-purpose tagging is defined in section <basic-link internal-destination="Simple-tables" text-decoration="underline">8 (Tables)</basic-link>.</block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">Lists of whatever kind can, of course, nest within list items to any depth required. Here, for example, a glossary list contains two items, each of which is itself a simple list: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">
               <inline>&lt;list type="gloss"&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;label&gt;</inline>EVIL<inline>&lt;/label&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;item&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;list type="simple"&gt;</inline>
   <inline>&lt;item&gt;</inline>I am cast upon a horrible desolate island, void of all hope of
       recovery.<inline>&lt;/item&gt;</inline>
   <inline>&lt;item&gt;</inline>I am singled out and separated as it were from all the world to be
       miserable.<inline>&lt;/item&gt;</inline>
   <inline>&lt;item&gt;</inline>I am divided from mankind — a solitaire; one banished from human
       society.<inline>&lt;/item&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;/list&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;/item&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;label&gt;</inline>GOOD<inline>&lt;/label&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;item&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;list type="simple"&gt;</inline>
   <inline>&lt;item&gt;</inline>But I am alive; and not drowned, as all my ship's company were.<inline>&lt;/item&gt;</inline>
   <inline>&lt;item&gt;</inline>But I am singled out, too, from all the ship's crew, to be spared from
       death...<inline>&lt;/item&gt;</inline>
   <inline>&lt;item&gt;</inline>But I am not starved, and perishing on a barren place, affording no
       sustenances....<inline>&lt;/item&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;/list&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;/item&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;/list&gt;</inline>
            </block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">Lists of bibliographic items should be tagged using the <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.listBibl" text-decoration="underline">&lt;listBibl&gt;</basic-link> element, described in the next section.</block>
         <block keep-with-next.within-page="always"
                id="Simple-bibls"
                text-align="left"
                font-family="Times"
                font-size="18pt"
                font-weight="bold"
                space-after="6pt"
                space-before.optimum="12pt"
                text-indent="0em">7. Bibliographic Citations</block>
         <block line-height="1">It is often useful to distinguish bibliographic citations where they occur within texts being transcribed for research, if only so that they will be properly formatted when the text is printed out. The element <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.bibl" text-decoration="underline">&lt;bibl&gt;</basic-link> is provided for this purpose. Where the components of a bibliographic reference are to be distinguished, the following elements may be used as appropriate. It is generally useful to distinguish at least those parts (such as the titles of articles, books, and journals) which will need special formatting. The other elements are provided for cases where particular interest attaches to such details: <list-block provisional-distance-between-starts="18pt"
                        provisional-label-separation="6pt"
                        text-indent="0em"
                        margin-right="10pt"
                        space-before="6pt"
                        space-after="6pt">
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.bibl" text-decoration="underline">bibl</basic-link>
                        </inline> (bibliographic citation) contains a loosely-structured bibliographic citation of which the sub-components may or may not be explicitly tagged.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.author" text-decoration="underline">author</basic-link>
                        </inline> in a bibliographic reference, contains the name(s) of an author, personal or corporate, of a work; for example in the same form as that provided by a recognized bibliographic name authority.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.biblScope" text-decoration="underline">biblScope</basic-link>
                        </inline> (scope of bibliographic reference) defines the scope of a bibliographic reference, for example as a list of page numbers, or a named subdivision of a larger work.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.date" text-decoration="underline">date</basic-link>
                        </inline> contains a date in any format.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.editor" text-decoration="underline">editor</basic-link>
                        </inline> contains a secondary statement of responsibility for a bibliographic item, for example the name of an individual, institution or organization, (or of several such) acting as editor, compiler, translator, etc.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.publisher" text-decoration="underline">publisher</basic-link>
                        </inline> provides the name of the organization responsible for the publication or distribution of a bibliographic item.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.pubPlace" text-decoration="underline">pubPlace</basic-link>
                        </inline> (publication place) contains the name of the place where a bibliographic item was published.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.relatedItem" text-decoration="underline">relatedItem</basic-link>
                        </inline> contains or references some other bibliographic item which is related to the present one in some specified manner, for example as a constituent or alternative version of it.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.title" text-decoration="underline">title</basic-link>
                        </inline> contains a title for any kind of work.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
            </list-block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">Consider, for example the following editorial note: <block text-align="start"
                   text-indent="0pt"
                   end-indent="12pt"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   font-size="9pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">He was a member of Parliament for Warwickshire in 1445, and died March 14, 1470 (according to Kittredge, <inline font-style="italic">Harvard Studies</inline> 5. 88ff).</block> This might be encoded as follows: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">He was a member of Parliament for Warwickshire
 in 1445, and died March 14, 1470 (according to <inline>&lt;bibl&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;author&gt;</inline>Kittredge<inline>&lt;/author&gt;</inline>,
<inline>&lt;title&gt;</inline>Harvard Studies<inline>&lt;/title&gt;</inline> 5. 88ff
<inline>&lt;/bibl&gt;</inline>).</block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">The bibliographic elements listed above are particularly useful in a born digital document which contains a bibliography encoded using the <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.listBibl" text-decoration="underline">&lt;listBibl&gt;</basic-link> element. Entries in the bibliography should be given an identifier, which can then be used as the target of cross references from elsewhere in the document: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">
               <inline>&lt;p&gt;</inline>Perec citing, amongst others <inline>&lt;ref target="#MK_73"&gt;</inline>Sturm und Drang, 1973<inline>&lt;/ref&gt;</inline>,
 concludes ... <inline>&lt;/p&gt;</inline>
            </block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">A <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.bibl" text-decoration="underline">&lt;bibl&gt;</basic-link> element may contain simply text, with possibly a few of its components distinguished by tagging, and much use of conventionalized punctuation, as in this example: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">
               <inline>&lt;bibl xml:id="MK_73"&gt;</inline>Sturm, U. &amp;amp; Drang, F. : <inline>&lt;title&gt;</inline>Musikalische
   Katastrophe<inline>&lt;/title&gt;</inline>. (Berlin, W. de Gruyter, 1973)<inline>&lt;/bibl&gt;</inline>
            </block> Alternatively, each of the components of the bibliographic reference may be clearly distinguished by tagging; in this case there is no requirement for conventionalized punctuation, since the processor will be able to generate this appropriately: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">
               <inline>&lt;bibl xml:id="MK73"&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;author&gt;</inline>Sturm, U.<inline>&lt;/author&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;author&gt;</inline>Drang, F.<inline>&lt;/author&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;title xml:lang="de" level="m"&gt;</inline>Musikalische Katastrophe<inline>&lt;/title&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;pubPlace&gt;</inline>Berlin<inline>&lt;/pubPlace&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;publisher&gt;</inline>W. de Gruyter<inline>&lt;/publisher&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;date&gt;</inline>1973<inline>&lt;/date&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;/bibl&gt;</inline>
            </block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">The element <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.biblFull" text-decoration="underline">&lt;biblFull&gt;</basic-link> is also provided for convience in cases where bibliographic citations following a more sophisticated model have been used; it is permitted only in the TEI header.  <list-block provisional-distance-between-starts="18pt"
                        provisional-label-separation="6pt"
                        text-indent="0em"
                        margin-right="10pt"
                        space-before="6pt"
                        space-after="6pt">
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.biblFull" text-decoration="underline">biblFull</basic-link>
                        </inline> (fully-structured bibliographic citation) contains a fully-structured bibliographic citation, in which all components of the TEI file description are present.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.listBibl" text-decoration="underline">listBibl</basic-link>
                        </inline> (citation list) contains a list of bibliographic citations of any kind.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
            </list-block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">The <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.listBibl" text-decoration="underline">&lt;listBibl&gt;</basic-link> element is used to group lists of bibliographic citations. It may contain a series of <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.bibl" text-decoration="underline">&lt;bibl&gt;</basic-link> or <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.biblFull" text-decoration="underline">&lt;biblFull&gt;</basic-link> elements.</block>
         <block keep-with-next.within-page="always"
                id="Simple-tables"
                text-align="left"
                font-family="Times"
                font-size="18pt"
                font-weight="bold"
                space-after="6pt"
                space-before.optimum="12pt"
                text-indent="0em">8. Tables</block>
         <block line-height="1">The following elements are provided for the description of tabular matter, commonly found in many kinds of narrative text. Note that TEI simplePrint provides no sophisticated ways of describing the detailed layout of a table beyond its organization into rows and columns.<list-block provisional-distance-between-starts="18pt"
                        provisional-label-separation="6pt"
                        text-indent="0em"
                        margin-right="10pt"
                        space-before="6pt"
                        space-after="6pt">
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.table" text-decoration="underline">table</basic-link>
                        </inline> contains text displayed in tabular form, in rows and columns.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.row" text-decoration="underline">row</basic-link>
                        </inline> contains one row of a table.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.cell" text-decoration="underline">cell</basic-link>
                        </inline> contains one cell of a table.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
            </list-block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">The <inline font-style="italic">role</inline> attribute may be used on either <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.cell" text-decoration="underline">&lt;cell&gt;</basic-link> or <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.row" text-decoration="underline">&lt;row&gt;</basic-link>to indicate the function of a cell, or of a row of cells. Its values should be taken from the following list: <list-block provisional-distance-between-starts="42pt"
                        provisional-label-separation="6pt"
                        text-indent="0em"
                        margin-right="10pt"
                        space-before="6pt"
                        space-after="6pt">
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="start"
                            font-weight="bold"
                            keep-together.within-line="always">
                        <inline>data</inline>
                     </block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <block> </block>data cell</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="start"
                            font-weight="bold"
                            keep-together.within-line="always">
                        <inline>label</inline>
                     </block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <block> </block>label cell</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="start"
                            font-weight="bold"
                            keep-together.within-line="always">
                        <inline>sum</inline>
                     </block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <block> </block>row or column sum data</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="start"
                            font-weight="bold"
                            keep-together.within-line="always">
                        <inline>total</inline>
                     </block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <block> </block>table total data</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
            </list-block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">For example, Defoe uses mortality tables like the following in the <inline font-style="italic">Journal of the Plague Year</inline> to show the rise and ebb of the epidemic: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">
               <inline>&lt;p&gt;</inline>It was indeed coming on amain, for the
 burials that same week were in the next adjoining parishes thus:— <inline>&lt;table rows="5" cols="4"&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;row role="data"&gt;</inline>
   <inline>&lt;cell role="label"&gt;</inline>St. Leonard's, Shoreditch<inline>&lt;/cell&gt;</inline>
   <inline>&lt;cell&gt;</inline>64<inline>&lt;/cell&gt;</inline>
   <inline>&lt;cell&gt;</inline>84<inline>&lt;/cell&gt;</inline>
   <inline>&lt;cell&gt;</inline>119<inline>&lt;/cell&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;/row&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;row role="data"&gt;</inline>
   <inline>&lt;cell role="label"&gt;</inline>St. Botolph's, Bishopsgate<inline>&lt;/cell&gt;</inline>
   <inline>&lt;cell&gt;</inline>65<inline>&lt;/cell&gt;</inline>
   <inline>&lt;cell&gt;</inline>105<inline>&lt;/cell&gt;</inline>
   <inline>&lt;cell&gt;</inline>116<inline>&lt;/cell&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;/row&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;row role="data"&gt;</inline>
   <inline>&lt;cell role="label"&gt;</inline>St. Giles's, Cripplegate<inline>&lt;/cell&gt;</inline>
   <inline>&lt;cell&gt;</inline>213<inline>&lt;/cell&gt;</inline>
   <inline>&lt;cell&gt;</inline>421<inline>&lt;/cell&gt;</inline>
   <inline>&lt;cell&gt;</inline>554<inline>&lt;/cell&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;/row&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;/table&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;/p&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;p&gt;</inline>This shutting up of houses was at first counted a very cruel and
 unchristian method, and the poor people so confined made bitter lamentations. ...
<inline>&lt;/p&gt;</inline>
            </block>
         </block>
         <block keep-with-next.within-page="always"
                id="Simple-figs"
                text-align="left"
                font-family="Times"
                font-size="18pt"
                font-weight="bold"
                space-after="6pt"
                space-before.optimum="12pt"
                text-indent="0em">9. Figures and Graphics</block>
         <block line-height="1">Not all the components of a document are necessarily textual. The most straightforward text will often contain diagrams or illustrations, to say nothing of documents in which image and text are inextricably intertwined, or electronic resources in which the two are complementary. </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">The encoder may simply record the presence of a graphic within the text, possibly with a brief description of its content, and may also provide a link to a digitized version of the graphic, using the following elements: <list-block provisional-distance-between-starts="18pt"
                        provisional-label-separation="6pt"
                        text-indent="0em"
                        margin-right="10pt"
                        space-before="6pt"
                        space-after="6pt">
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.graphic" text-decoration="underline">graphic</basic-link>
                        </inline> indicates the location of a graphic or illustration, either forming part of a text, or providing an image of it.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.figure" text-decoration="underline">figure</basic-link>
                        </inline> groups elements representing or containing graphic information such as an illustration, formula, or figure.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.figDesc" text-decoration="underline">figDesc</basic-link>
                        </inline> (description of figure) contains a brief prose description of the appearance or content of a graphic figure, for use when documenting an image without displaying it.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
            </list-block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">Any textual information accompanying the graphic, such as a heading and/or caption, may be included within the <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.figure" text-decoration="underline">&lt;figure&gt;</basic-link> element itself, in a <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.head" text-decoration="underline">&lt;head&gt;</basic-link> and one or more <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.p" text-decoration="underline">&lt;p&gt;</basic-link> elements, as may any text appearing within the graphic itself. It is strongly recommended that a prose description of the image be supplied, as the content of a <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.figDesc" text-decoration="underline">&lt;figDesc&gt;</basic-link> element, for the use of applications which are not able to render the graphic, and to render the document accessible to vision-impaired readers. (Such text is not normally considered part of the document proper.)</block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">The simplest use for these elements is to mark the position of a graphic and provide a link to it, as in this example: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">
               <inline>&lt;pb n="412"/&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;figure&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;graphic url="./../Images/p412fig.png"
  width="40%"/&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;/figure&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;pb n="413"/&gt;</inline>
            </block> This indicates that the graphic contained by the file <inline color="black" font-family="Helvetica">p412fig.png</inline> appears between pages 412 and 413.</block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">The <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.graphic" text-decoration="underline">&lt;graphic&gt;</basic-link> element can appear anywhere that textual content is permitted, within but not between paragraphs or headings. In the following example, the encoder has decided to treat a specific printer's ornament as a heading: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">
               <inline>&lt;head&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;graphic url="http://www.iath.virginia.edu/gants/Ornaments/Heads/hp-ral02.gif"/&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;/head&gt;</inline>
            </block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">More usually, a graphic will have at the least an identifying title, which may be encoded using the <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.head" text-decoration="underline">&lt;head&gt;</basic-link> element, or a number of figures may be grouped together in a particular structure, as in the following example: <block text-align="center" id="d27e3364">
               <external-graphic id="d27e3365"
                                 src="url(./../Images/fezziPic.png)"
                                 content-width="scale-to-fit"
                                 content-height="scale-to-fit"
                                 width="62%"/>
            </block>
            <block text-align="center"
                   font-style="italic"
                   end-indent="12pt"
                   start-indent="12pt">Figure 3. </block> The <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.figure" text-decoration="underline">&lt;figure&gt;</basic-link> element provides a means of wrapping one or more such elements together as a kind of graphic ‘block’. It may also include a brief description of the image: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">
               <inline>&lt;figure&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;graphic url="./../Images/fezzipic.png"/&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;head&gt;</inline>Mr Fezziwig's Ball<inline>&lt;/head&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;figDesc&gt;</inline>A Cruikshank engraving showing Mr Fezziwig leading a group of
   revellers.<inline>&lt;/figDesc&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;/figure&gt;</inline>
            </block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">These cases should be carefully distinguished from the case where an encoded text is complemented by a collection of digital images, maintained as a distinct resource. The <inline font-style="italic">facs</inline> attribute may be used to associate any element in an encoded text with a digital facsimile of it. In the simplest case, the <inline font-style="italic">facs</inline> attribute on the <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.pb" text-decoration="underline">&lt;pb&gt;</basic-link> element may be used to supply a location for an image file corresponding with that point in the text: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">
               <inline>&lt;div&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;pb facs="page1.png" n="1"/&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;!-- text contained on page 1 is encoded here --&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;pb facs="page2.png" n="2"/&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;!-- text contained on page 2 is encoded here --&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;/div&gt;</inline>
            </block> This method is only appropriate in the simple case where each digital image file <inline color="black" font-family="Helvetica">page1.png</inline> etc. corresponds with a single transcribed and encoded page. If multiple images are provided for each page, or more detailed alignment of image and transcription is required, for example because the image files actually represent double page spreads, more sophisticated mechanisms are needed, as further discussed in <basic-link internal-destination="Simple-fax" text-decoration="underline">14 (Encoding a Digital Facsimile)</basic-link> below.</block>
         <block keep-with-next.within-page="always"
                id="Simple-anal"
                text-align="left"
                font-family="Times"
                font-size="18pt"
                font-weight="bold"
                space-after="6pt"
                space-before.optimum="12pt"
                text-indent="0em">10. Analysis</block>
         <block keep-with-next.within-page="always"
                id="d27e3407"
                text-align="left"
                font-family="Times"
                font-size="14pt"
                font-weight="bold"
                space-after="3pt"
                space-before.optimum="9pt"
                text-indent="0em">10.1. Orthographic Sentences</block>
         <block line-height="1">Interpretation typically ranges across the whole of a text, with no particular respect to other structural units. A useful preliminary to intensive interpretation is therefore to segment the text into discrete and identifiable units, each of which can then bear a label for use as a sort of ‘canonical reference’. To facilitate such uses, these units may not cross each other, nor nest within each other. They may conveniently be represented using the following element: <list-block provisional-distance-between-starts="18pt"
                        provisional-label-separation="6pt"
                        text-indent="0em"
                        margin-right="10pt"
                        space-before="6pt"
                        space-after="6pt">
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.s" text-decoration="underline">s</basic-link>
                        </inline> (s-unit) contains a sentence-like division of a text.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
            </list-block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">As the name suggests, the <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.s" text-decoration="underline">&lt;s&gt;</basic-link> element is most commonly used (in linguistic applications at least) for marking <inline font-style="italic" font-weight="normal">orthographic sentences</inline>, that is, units defined by orthographic features such as punctuation. For example, the passage from <inline font-style="italic">Jane Eyre</inline> discussed earlier might be divided into s-units as follows: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">
               <inline>&lt;div type="chapter" n="38"&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;pb n="474"/&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;p&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;s n="001"&gt;</inline>Reader, I married him.<inline>&lt;/s&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;s n="002"&gt;</inline>A quiet wedding we had:<inline>&lt;/s&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;s n="003"&gt;</inline>he and I, the parson and clerk, were alone present.<inline>&lt;/s&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;s n="004"&gt;</inline>When
     we got back from church, I went into the kitchen of the manor-house, where Mary
     was cooking the dinner, and John cleaning the knives, and I said —<inline>&lt;/s&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;/p&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;p&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;q&gt;</inline>
   <inline>&lt;s n="005"&gt;</inline>Mary, I have been married to Mr Rochester this morning.<inline>&lt;/s&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;/q&gt;</inline> ...
 <inline>&lt;/p&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;/div&gt;</inline>
            </block> Note that <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.s" text-decoration="underline">&lt;s&gt;</basic-link> elements cannot nest: the beginning of one <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.s" text-decoration="underline">&lt;s&gt;</basic-link> element implies that the previous one has finished. When s-units are tagged as shown above, it is advisable to tag the entire text end-to-end, so that every word in the text being analyzed will be contained by exactly one <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.s" text-decoration="underline">&lt;s&gt;</basic-link> element, whose identifier can then be used to specify a unique reference for it. If the identifiers used are unique within the document, then the <inline font-style="italic">xml:id</inline> attribute might be used in preference to the <inline font-style="italic">n</inline> attribute used in the above example.</block>
         <block keep-with-next.within-page="always"
                id="d27e3469"
                text-align="left"
                font-family="Times"
                font-size="14pt"
                font-weight="bold"
                space-after="3pt"
                space-before.optimum="9pt"
                text-indent="0em">10.2. Words and Punctuation</block>
         <block line-height="1">Tokenization, that is, the identification of lexical or non-lexical tokens within a text, is a very common requirement for all kinds of textual analysis, and not an entirely trivial one. The decision as to whether, for example, ‘can't’ in English or ‘du’ in French should be treated as one word or two is not simple. Consequently it is often useful to make explicit the preferred tokenization in a marked up text. The following elements are available for this purpose: <list-block provisional-distance-between-starts="18pt"
                        provisional-label-separation="6pt"
                        text-indent="0em"
                        margin-right="10pt"
                        space-before="6pt"
                        space-after="6pt">
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.w" text-decoration="underline">w</basic-link>
                        </inline> (word) represents a grammatical (not necessarily orthographic) word.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.c" text-decoration="underline">c</basic-link>
                        </inline> (character) represents a character.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.pc" text-decoration="underline">pc</basic-link>
                        </inline> (punctuation character) contains a character or string of characters regarded as constituting a single punctuation mark.</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
            </list-block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">For example, the output from a part of speech tagger might be recorded in TEI simplePrint as follows: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">
               <inline>&lt;s n="1"&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;w ana="#NP0"&gt;</inline>Marley<inline>&lt;/w&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;w ana="#VBD"&gt;</inline>was<inline>&lt;/w&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;w ana="#AJ0"&gt;</inline>dead<inline>&lt;/w&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;pc&gt;</inline>:<inline>&lt;/pc&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;w ana="#TO0"&gt;</inline>to<inline>&lt;/w&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;w ana="#VBB"&gt;</inline>begin<inline>&lt;/w&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;w ana="#PRP"&gt;</inline>with<inline>&lt;/w&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;pc ana="#SENT"&gt;</inline>.<inline>&lt;/pc&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;/s&gt;</inline>
            </block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">In this example, each token in the input has been decorated with an automatically generated part of speech code, using the <inline font-style="italic">ana</inline> attribute discussed in section <basic-link internal-destination="xatts" text-decoration="underline">3.7.3 (Special Kinds of Linking)</basic-link> above. The system has also distinguished between tokens to be treated as words (tagged <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.w" text-decoration="underline">&lt;w&gt;</basic-link>) and tokens considered to be punctuation (tagged <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.pc" text-decoration="underline">&lt;pc&gt;</basic-link>). It may also sometimes be useful to distinguish tokens which consist of a single letter or character: the <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.c" text-decoration="underline">&lt;c&gt;</basic-link> element is provided for this purpose. </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">The <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.w" text-decoration="underline">&lt;w&gt;</basic-link> also provides for each word to be associated with a root form or lemma, either explicitly using the <inline font-style="italic">lemma</inline> attribute, or by reference, using the <inline font-style="italic">lemmaRef</inline> attribute, as in this example: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">...<inline>&lt;w ana="#VBD" lemma="be"
 lemmaRef="http://www.myLexicon.com/be"&gt;</inline>was<inline>&lt;/w&gt;</inline> ... </block>
         </block>
         <block keep-with-next.within-page="always"
                id="d27e3566"
                text-align="left"
                font-family="Times"
                font-size="14pt"
                font-weight="bold"
                space-after="3pt"
                space-before.optimum="9pt"
                text-indent="0em">10.3. General-Purpose Interpretation Elements</block>
         <block line-height="1">The <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.w" text-decoration="underline">&lt;w&gt;</basic-link> element is a specialisation of the <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.seg" text-decoration="underline">&lt;seg&gt;</basic-link> element which has already been introduced for use in identifying otherwise unmarked targets of cross references and hypertext links (see section <basic-link internal-destination="Simple-ptrs" text-decoration="underline">3.7 (Cross References and Links)</basic-link>); it can be used to distinguish any portion of text to which the encoder wishes to assign a user-specified <inline font-style="italic">type</inline> or a unique identifier; it may thus be used to tag textual features for which there is no other provision in the published TEI Guidelines.</block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">For example, the TEI Guidelines provide no ‘apostrophe’ element to mark parts of a literary text in which the narrator addresses the reader (or hearer) directly. One approach might be to regard these as instances of the <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.q" text-decoration="underline">&lt;q&gt;</basic-link> element, distinguished from others by an appropriate value for the <inline font-style="italic">who</inline> attribute. A possibly simpler, and certainly more general, solution would however be to use the <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.seg" text-decoration="underline">&lt;seg&gt;</basic-link> element as follows: <block font-family="Courier"
                   background-color="lightgray"
                   color="black"
                   white-space-treatment="preserve"
                   linefeed-treatment="preserve"
                   white-space-collapse="false"
                   wrap-option="no-wrap"
                   text-indent="0em"
                   hyphenate="false"
                   start-indent="12pt"
                   text-align="start"
                   font-size="6pt"
                   space-before.optimum="4pt"
                   space-after.optimum="4pt">
               <inline>&lt;div type="chapter" n="38"&gt;</inline>
 <inline>&lt;p&gt;</inline>
  <inline>&lt;seg type="apostrophe"&gt;</inline>Reader, I married him.<inline>&lt;/seg&gt;</inline> A quiet wedding we had:
   ...<inline>&lt;/p&gt;</inline>
               <inline>&lt;/div&gt;</inline>
            </block> The <inline font-style="italic">type</inline> attribute on the <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.seg" text-decoration="underline">&lt;seg&gt;</basic-link> element can take any value, and so can be used to distinguish phrase-level phenomena of any kind; it is good practice to record the values used and their significance in the TEI header or in the documentation of the encoding system. </block>
         <block keep-with-next.within-page="always"
                id="d27e3610"
                text-align="left"
                font-family="Times"
                font-size="18pt"
                font-weight="bold"
                space-after="6pt"
                space-before.optimum="12pt"
                text-indent="0em">11. Common Attributes</block>
         <block line-height="1">Some attributes are available on many elements, though not on all. These attributes are defined using a TEI attribute class, a concept which is discussed further in the TEI Guidelines. We list here some attribute classes which have been adapted or customized for use in TEI simplePrint. </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">The elements <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.add" text-decoration="underline">&lt;add&gt;</basic-link>, <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.figure" text-decoration="underline">&lt;figure&gt;</basic-link>, <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.fw" text-decoration="underline">&lt;fw&gt;</basic-link>, <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.label" text-decoration="underline">&lt;label&gt;</basic-link>, <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.note" text-decoration="underline">&lt;note&gt;</basic-link> and <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.stage" text-decoration="underline">&lt;stage&gt;</basic-link> all take the attribute <inline font-style="italic">place</inline> to indicate whereabouts on the page they appear. In TEI simplePrint the possible values for this attribute are limited as indicated below: <list-block provisional-distance-between-starts="18pt"
                        provisional-label-separation="6pt"
                        text-indent="0em"
                        margin-right="10pt"
                        space-before="6pt"
                        space-after="6pt">
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.att.placement" text-decoration="underline">att.placement</basic-link>
                        </inline> provides attributes for describing where on the source page or object a textual element appears.<table text-align="left"
                               font-size="9pt"
                               table-layout="fixed"
                               width="100%"
                               id="d27e3646">
                           <table-column column-number="1" column-width="12.195121951219512%"/>
                           <table-column column-number="2" column-width="87.8048780487805%"/>
                           <table-body text-indent="0pt">
                              <table-row>
                                 <table-cell padding="2pt" text-align="left">
                                    <block>
                                       <inline font-style="italic">place</inline>
                                    </block>
                                 </table-cell>
                                 <table-cell padding="2pt" text-align="left">
                                    <block>specifies where this item is placed.</block>
                                 </table-cell>
                              </table-row>
                           </table-body>
                        </table>
                     </block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
            </list-block>
            <list-block provisional-distance-between-starts="42pt"
                        provisional-label-separation="6pt"
                        text-indent="0em"
                        margin-right="10pt"
                        space-before="6pt"
                        space-after="6pt">
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="start"
                            font-weight="bold"
                            keep-together.within-line="always">above</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <block> </block>above the line</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="start"
                            font-weight="bold"
                            keep-together.within-line="always">below</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <block> </block>below the line</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="start"
                            font-weight="bold"
                            keep-together.within-line="always">top</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <block> </block>at the top of the page</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="start"
                            font-weight="bold"
                            keep-together.within-line="always">top-right</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <block> </block>at the top right of the page</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="start"
                            font-weight="bold"
                            keep-together.within-line="always">top-left</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <block> </block>at the top left of the page</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="start"
                            font-weight="bold"
                            keep-together.within-line="always">top-centre</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <block> </block>at the top center of the page</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="start"
                            font-weight="bold"
                            keep-together.within-line="always">bottom-right</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <block> </block>at the bottom right of the page</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="start"
                            font-weight="bold"
                            keep-together.within-line="always">bottom-left</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <block> </block>at the bottom left of the page</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="start"
                            font-weight="bold"
                            keep-together.within-line="always">bottom-centre</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <block> </block>at the bottom centre of the page</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="start"
                            font-weight="bold"
                            keep-together.within-line="always">bottom</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <block> </block>at the foot of the page</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="start"
                            font-weight="bold"
                            keep-together.within-line="always">tablebottom</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <block> </block>underneath a table</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="start"
                            font-weight="bold"
                            keep-together.within-line="always">margin-right</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <block> </block>in the right-hand margin</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="start"
                            font-weight="bold"
                            keep-together.within-line="always">margin</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <block> </block>in the outer margin</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="start"
                            font-weight="bold"
                            keep-together.within-line="always">margin-inner</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <block> </block>in the inner margin</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="start"
                            font-weight="bold"
                            keep-together.within-line="always">margin-left</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <block> </block>in the left-hand margin</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="start"
                            font-weight="bold"
                            keep-together.within-line="always">opposite</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <block> </block>on the opposite, i.e. facing, page</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="start"
                            font-weight="bold"
                            keep-together.within-line="always">overleaf</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <block> </block>on the other side of the leaf</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="start"
                            font-weight="bold"
                            keep-together.within-line="always">overstrike</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <block> </block>superimposed on top of the current context</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="start"
                            font-weight="bold"
                            keep-together.within-line="always">end</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <block> </block>at the end of the volume</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="start"
                            font-weight="bold"
                            keep-together.within-line="always">divend</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <block> </block>at the end of the current division</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="start"
                            font-weight="bold"
                            keep-together.within-line="always">parend</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <block> </block>at the end of the current paragraph</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="start"
                            font-weight="bold"
                            keep-together.within-line="always">inline</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <block> </block>within the body of the text</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="start"
                            font-weight="bold"
                            keep-together.within-line="always">inspace</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <block> </block>in a predefined space, for example left by an earlier scribe</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="start"
                            font-weight="bold"
                            keep-together.within-line="always">block</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <block> </block>formatted as an indented paragraph</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
            </list-block>
         </block>
         <block line-height="1"
                text-indent="1em"
                space-before.optimum="0pt"
                space-before.maximum="12pt">The elements <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.add" text-decoration="underline">&lt;add&gt;</basic-link>, <inline hyphenate="false" color="black" font-family="Courier">&lt;am&gt;</inline>, <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.corr" text-decoration="underline">&lt;corr&gt;</basic-link>, <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.date" text-decoration="underline">&lt;date&gt;</basic-link>, <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.del" text-decoration="underline">&lt;del&gt;</basic-link>, <inline hyphenate="false" color="black" font-family="Courier">&lt;ex&gt;</inline>, <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.expan" text-decoration="underline">&lt;expan&gt;</basic-link>, <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.gap" text-decoration="underline">&lt;gap&gt;</basic-link>, <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.name" text-decoration="underline">&lt;name&gt;</basic-link>, <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.reg" text-decoration="underline">&lt;reg&gt;</basic-link>, <inline hyphenate="false" color="black" font-family="Courier">&lt;space&gt;</inline>, <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.subst" text-decoration="underline">&lt;subst&gt;</basic-link>, <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.supplied" text-decoration="underline">&lt;supplied&gt;</basic-link>, <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.time" text-decoration="underline">&lt;time&gt;</basic-link> and <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.unclear" text-decoration="underline">&lt;unclear&gt;</basic-link> all use the attribute <inline font-style="italic">unit</inline> to indicate the units in which the size of the feature concerned is expressed. In TEI simplePrint the possible values for this attribute are limited as indicated below: <list-block provisional-distance-between-starts="18pt"
                        provisional-label-separation="6pt"
                        text-indent="0em"
                        margin-right="10pt"
                        space-before="6pt"
                        space-after="6pt">
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="end">•</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <inline font-weight="bold">
                           <basic-link internal-destination="TEI.att.dimensions" text-decoration="underline">att.dimensions</basic-link>
                        </inline> provides attributes for describing the size of physical objects.<table text-align="left"
                               font-size="9pt"
                               table-layout="fixed"
                               width="100%"
                               id="d27e3767">
                           <table-body text-indent="0pt">
                              <table-row>
                                 <table-cell padding="2pt" text-align="left">
                                    <block>
                                       <inline font-style="italic">unit</inline>
                                    </block>
                                 </table-cell>
                                 <table-cell padding="2pt" text-align="left">
                                    <block>names the unit used for the measurement</block>
                                 </table-cell>
                              </table-row>
                           </table-body>
                        </table>
                     </block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
            </list-block>
            <list-block provisional-distance-between-starts="42pt"
                        provisional-label-separation="6pt"
                        text-indent="0em"
                        margin-right="10pt"
                        space-before="6pt"
                        space-after="6pt">
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="start"
                            font-weight="bold"
                            keep-together.within-line="always">chars</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <block> </block>characters</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="start"
                            font-weight="bold"
                            keep-together.within-line="always">lines</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <block> </block>lines</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="start"
                            font-weight="bold"
                            keep-together.within-line="always">pages</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <block> </block>pages</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="start"
                            font-weight="bold"
                            keep-together.within-line="always">words</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <block> </block>words</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="start"
                            font-weight="bold"
                            keep-together.within-line="always">cm</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <block> </block>centimetres</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="start"
                            font-weight="bold"
                            keep-together.within-line="always">mm</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <block> </block>millimetres</block>
                  </list-item-body>
               </list-item>
               <list-item space-before.optimum="4pt">
                  <list-item-label end-indent="label-end()">
                     <block text-align="start"
                            font-weight="bold"
                            keep-together.within-line="always">in</block>
                  </list-item-label>
                  <list-item-body start-indent="body-start()">
                     <block font-weight="normal">
                        <block> </block>inches</block>
                  </list-item-body>
